@

A root is a chunk that is defined but never used; it gets a shadow
when its inferred language has a configuration.  Both facts come
from the Vim script side through the accessors, so there is exactly
one inventory and one inference.

<<shadow: discover the typed roots>>=
local function typed_roots(nwbuf)
  local occ, langs
  vim.api.nvim_buf_call(nwbuf, function()
    occ = vim.fn['noweb#occurrences']()
    langs = vim.fn['noweb#languages']()
  end)
  local cfg = language_config()
  local roots = {}
  for name, entry in pairs(occ) do
    if #entry.defs > 0 and #entry.uses == 0 then
      local lang = langs[name]
      if lang and cfg[lang] then
        roots[name] = { lang = lang, cfg = cfg[lang] }
      end
    end
  end
  return roots
end
@

Tangling feeds the \emph{live buffer} to notangle on stdin, so
unsaved chunks are part of the shadow the moment they are typed.
The leader is single-quoted inside the filter string because
notangle hands the filter to a shell (an unquoted [[#]] would start
a comment there).  The call is synchronous; notangle on a large
source is a few milliseconds, and sync is memoized and debounced.
The exit status is advisory: a use of an undefined chunk---legal
noweb, and common in a document still being written---makes
notangle warn and exit non-zero while tangling the chunk as empty,
and losing every shadow over it would punish exactly the moment
the tooling helps most.  Only a run with no output at all fails.

Note the absent [[-t8]]: the shadow should show the language server
what the \emph{build} will produce, and notangle's default---tabs
expanded---is what build setups overwhelmingly tangle with.  Dogfood
proved the point: with [[-t8]] the shadow of a real Python source
preserved literal tabs its build would have expanded, and the server
reported a [[TabError]] the shipped program does not have.  A
language whose build does tangle differently can say so:
[[tangle]] in its [[g:noweb_languages]] entry is a list of extra
notangle arguments.  (The maps are indifferent either way---the
column correction reads the actual leading whitespace of both
lines.)

Note also what does \emph{not} have to be arranged: linemark is named
here as a filter and nowhere invoked, so it never needs to be on the
user's [[PATH]].  notangle appends its own [[LIB]] directory to
[[PATH]] before running a filter, and [[LIB]] is where
[[contrib/dbosk]]'s [[make install]] puts linemark---so a normal
install is enough.  nolinemap is the opposite case: the
plugin execs it directly (\cref{sec:nowebmake}), and it does have to
be findable.

<<shadow: tangle one root with markers>>=
local function tangle(nwbuf, root, cfg)
  local lines = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(nwbuf, 0, -1, false)
  local argv = { 'notangle' }
  vim.list_extend(argv, cfg.tangle or {})
  vim.list_extend(argv, {
    '-filter', 'linemark ' .. eager_flag() .. "-c '" .. cfg.leader .. "'",
    '-R' .. root,
  })
  local res = vim.system(argv,
    { stdin = table.concat(lines, '\n') .. '\n', text = true }):wait(5000)
  -- notangle warns and exits non-zero on an undefined chunk, but still
  -- tangles it as empty; a document in progress keeps its shadows, so
  -- only a run that produced no output at all counts as failed.
  if not res or (res.code ~= 0 and (res.stdout or '') == '') then
    return nil
  end
  local out = vim.split(res.stdout or '', '\n')
  if out[#out] == '' then
    table.remove(out)
  end
  return rejoin_splices(out, cfg.leader)
end
@

Linemark plants a chunk's start marker at the \emph{definition}
site, so it rides along into every splice.  Spliced on a line of
its own---the overwhelmingly common whole-line use---the marker
lands on a line of its own, a legal comment.  But a chunk used
\emph{mid-line}, as in

\begin{minted}{bash}
  | if [ @<<test for programming terms@>> ]; then
\end{minted}

\noindent
drags the marker into the middle of that line, where its comment
leader swallows the rest---and since a marker only means anything
at a line boundary, linemark must break the line after it.  The
splice that plain notangle joins seamlessly comes out split in
two, re-indented, with a marker wedged between: no longer the
program the build produces, and for many languages no longer a
program at all (the shell in the example loses [[if]]'s condition
to a comment).  The language server then reports errors the real
program does not have, and the marker line, being glued to code,
escapes the preview's whole-line filter.

The repair below undoes exactly what linemark did: an inline
marker moves onto a line of its own \emph{before} the junction,
and the two halves of the broken line are joined back together,
stripping the indentation notangle added for what it took to be a
continuation line (the width of the code before the marker,
tab-aware at notangle's default tab stops of eight).  The result
is byte-identical to the plain tangle except for whole-line
markers, everywhere.  The maps stay exact too, with one nuance:
the joined line carries text from two source lines---the use line
and the chunk's first---and one line can only map to one.  The
marker parser below settles that in the use line's favor whenever
its text visibly lives on in the junction.

The relocated marker also keeps the tangle self-describing for
\texttt{nolinemap}, which derives the same map from the same
markers when [[:NowebMake]] runs a compiler over it
(\cref{sec:nowebmake}).

Newer linemark, prompted by this very repair, defaults to a
\emph{safe} placement that never breaks a spliced line: a chunk's
first marker follows its first line, and no marker lands after a
backslash-continued one.  Good for raw consumers, but the safety is
bought with anchoring---lines skipped over anchor across the splice
by counting, and a continuation-heavy chunk like edumail's grep
pipeline would lose the exact per-line map the repair recovers.
Since we \emph{are} a consumer that repairs junctions, the shadows
ask linemark for the old placement with [[-e]]: every marker
present, every line anchored, every break mended here.  Whether the
installed linemark knows the flag is probed once, by tangling a
one-chunk document through it; an older linemark fails the probe
and is simply eager by default, so both answers lead to the same
stream.

<<shadow: request eager markers>>=
local eager
local function eager_flag()
  if eager == nil then
    local res = vim.system(
      { 'notangle', '-filter', 'linemark -e', '-Rprobe' },
      { stdin = '@<<probe@>>=\nx\n@\n', text = true }):wait(2000)
    eager = (res and res.code == 0) and '-e ' or ''
  end
  return eager
end
@

<<shadow: rejoin mid-line splices>>=
local function display_width(s)
  local w = 0
  for i = 1, #s do
    w = s:byte(i) == 9 and w + 8 - w % 8 or w + 1
  end
  return w
end

local function strip_columns(line, width)
  local col, i = 0, 1
  while col < width and i <= #line do
    local b = line:byte(i)
    if b == 9 then
      col = col + 8 - col % 8
    elseif b == 32 then
      col = col + 1
    else
      break
    end
    i = i + 1
  end
  return line:sub(i)
end

local function rejoin_splices(lines, leader)
  local inline = '^(.-)(' .. vim.pesc(leader) .. ' %d+ "[^"]*")%s*$'
  local out = {}
  local i = 1
  while i <= #lines do
    local prefix, mark = lines[i]:match(inline)
    if prefix and prefix:find('%S') and i < #lines then
      -- A marker spliced into the middle of a line: give it a line
      -- of its own and mend the line linemark broke, undoing the
      -- continuation indent notangle added to the second half.
      table.insert(out, mark)
      lines[i + 1] = prefix
        .. strip_columns(lines[i + 1], display_width(prefix))
    else
      table.insert(out, lines[i])
    end
    i = i + 1
  end
  return out
end
@

A marker line announces the source line of the tangled line that
follows; both the marker and that line map to it, and subsequent
lines count up until the next marker.  After the rejoining above,
every marker stands on a line of its own.  Markers are indented
with their splice, hence the leading-whitespace allowance.

The parser also decides which markers may \emph{stay} in the text.
Eager linemark marks every discontinuity, including one it cannot
mark safely: a re-anchor after a use line ending in a backslash
lands between that line and its continuation, severing them.  (This
also lurked, unrepaired, in the shadows of earlier versions.)  Such
a marker is left out of the shadow text---the continuation stays
whole---but its announcement still anchors the map, which is the
whole point of asking for eager markers: the information arrives
even where the comment cannot.  The function therefore returns the
buffer content alongside the maps, with [[src]], [[fwd]] and
[[marker]] keyed by the lines the buffer actually gets.

One refinement, and it is what makes the maps usable around
mid-line uses.  A marker that interrupts the running count marks a
chunk use, and the source line it interrupted---the use line---may
live on in the tangled line after the marker: the rejoined
junction starts with the text the use line put before the
reference, and a chunk of a single line gets the use line's tail
glued on.  Attributing that line to the announced chunk line, as
the plain counting would, orphans the use line entirely---and a
chunk like

\begin{minted}{text}
@<<grep@>>=
grep -qiEo
@
\end{minted}

\noindent
used once per pattern line makes it absurd: six tangled lines all
mapping to the one-line definition, six use lines mapping nowhere,
and the preview cursor going still over the whole block.  So when
the use line visibly lives on in the junction---text before the
reference, or a tail glued to a single-line chunk (the tangled
line after the junction is a marker again)---the junction maps to
the \emph{use} line.  A whole-line use contributes nothing to its
splice, and the splice stays the chunk's, exactly as before.
Nested splices can stack several interrupted lines onto one
junction (a use expanding a chunk whose first line is again a
use); each of them gets its forward mapping into the junction, and
the reverse map goes to the innermost, whose text dominates the
line.

<<shadow: parse the markers into line maps>>=
local function use_lives_on(useline, nextline, pat)
  if not useline or useline:match('^@<<.-@>>=%s*$') then
    return false
  end
  local prefix, tail = useline:match('^(.-)@<<.-@>>(.*)$')
  if not prefix then
    return false
  end
  return prefix:find('%S') ~= nil
    or (tail:find('%S') ~= nil
        and nextline ~= nil and nextline:match(pat) ~= nil)
end

local function parse_maps(lines, leader, srclines)
  local pat = '^%s*' .. vim.pesc(leader) .. ' (%d+) "'
  local out, src, fwd, marker = {}, {}, {}, {}
  local cur = nil
  local pending = {}
  for t, line in ipairs(lines) do
    local announced = line:match(pat)
    if announced then
      if cur then
        table.insert(pending, cur)
      end
      cur = tonumber(announced)
      local prev = out[#out]
      if not (prev and prev:match('\\$')) then
        -- a marker after a backslash-continued line would sever the
        -- continuation: leave it out of the text, keep its
        -- announcement for the map
        table.insert(out, line)
        marker[#out] = true
        src[#out] = cur
      end
    elseif cur then
      table.insert(out, line)
      local o = #out
      src[o] = cur
      for _, u in ipairs(pending) do
        if use_lives_on(srclines[u], lines[t + 1], pat) then
          src[o] = u
          if not fwd[u] then
            fwd[u] = o
          end
        end
      end
      pending = {}
      if not fwd[cur] then
        fwd[cur] = o
      end
      cur = cur + 1
    else
      table.insert(out, line)
    end
  end
  return out, src, fwd, marker
end
@

The shadow buffer is an unlisted scratch buffer named after the
source and the root, so buffer lists and language servers can tell
shadows apart.  Setting its filetype is deliberately more than
cosmetic: the LSP client derives the document's language from it,
and the language's own ftplugins run on the hidden buffer.  The
server, when configured, is started with the noweb file's directory
as root; [[vim.lsp.start]] reuses a running client for the same
name and root.

<<shadow: the hidden buffer and its language server>>=
local function shadow_name(nwbuf, root)
  local nwname = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_name(nwbuf)
  return nwname .. '.shadow/' .. root:gsub('[%[%]]', '')
end

local function ensure_shadow(nwbuf, root, lang, cfg)
  local st = state[nwbuf]
  local sh = st.roots[root]
  if not (sh and vim.api.nvim_buf_is_valid(sh.buf)) then
    local buf = vim.api.nvim_create_buf(false, true)
    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_name(buf, shadow_name(nwbuf, root))
    sh = { buf = buf, root = root, lang = lang, cfg = cfg,
           ns = vim.api.nvim_create_namespace('noweb-shadow:' .. root) }
    st.roots[root] = sh
    vim.bo[buf].filetype = cfg.filetype
    -- Defaults name a server per language; only attach the ones that
    -- are actually installed, so the defaults are harmless everywhere.
    if cfg.lsp and vim.fn.executable(cfg.lsp[1]) == 1 then
      vim.lsp.start({
        name = 'noweb-' .. lang,
        cmd = cfg.lsp,
        root_dir = vim.fs.dirname(vim.api.nvim_buf_get_name(nwbuf)),
      }, { bufnr = buf })
    end
    <<shadow: forward this shadow's diagnostics>>
  end
  return sh
end
@

Diagnostics translate line by line: the diagnostic's tangled line
looks up its source line, the column moves by the difference in
leading whitespace (the splice indent), and diagnostics on marker
lines---lines the author never wrote---are dropped.  The translated
set is published on the noweb buffer in the shadow's own namespace,
so several roots coexist.

<<shadow: forward diagnostics into the noweb buffer>>=
local function indent_width(line)
  return #(line:match('^%s*') or '')
end

local function col_delta(sh, t, nwbuf)
  local tline = (vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(sh.buf, t - 1, t, false)[1]) or ''
  local nline = (vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(
    nwbuf, sh.src[t] - 1, sh.src[t], false)[1]) or ''
  return indent_width(tline) - indent_width(nline)
end

local function forward_diagnostics(nwbuf, sh)
  local out = {}
  for _, d in ipairs(vim.diagnostic.get(sh.buf)) do
    local t = d.lnum + 1
    if sh.src[t] and not sh.marker[t] then
      local delta = col_delta(sh, t, nwbuf)
      local nd = vim.tbl_extend('force', {}, d)
      nd.bufnr = nwbuf
      nd.lnum = sh.src[t] - 1
      nd.col = math.max(0, d.col - delta)
      if nd.end_lnum and sh.src[nd.end_lnum + 1] then
        nd.end_lnum = sh.src[nd.end_lnum + 1] - 1
        nd.end_col = math.max(0, (nd.end_col or 0) - delta)
      else
        nd.end_lnum = nd.lnum
        nd.end_col = nd.col
      end
      table.insert(out, nd)
    end
  end
  vim.diagnostic.set(sh.ns, nwbuf, out)
end
@

<<shadow: forward this shadow's diagnostics>>=
vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('DiagnosticChanged', {
  buffer = sh.buf,
  callback = function()
    if vim.api.nvim_buf_is_valid(nwbuf) then
      forward_diagnostics(nwbuf, sh)
    end
  end,
})
@

Sync re-tangles every typed root whose buffer changed since the last
sync, updates the shadow buffers (which sends the language servers
their [[didChange]]), rebuilds the maps, and drops shadows whose
root disappeared.

<<shadow: sync>>=
function M.sync(nwbuf)
  nwbuf = (nwbuf and nwbuf ~= 0) and nwbuf or vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
  local st = state[nwbuf]
  if not st then
    return nil
  end
  local tick = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_changedtick(nwbuf)
  if st.tick == tick then
    return st
  end
  st.tick = tick
  local roots = typed_roots(nwbuf)
  for root, sh in pairs(st.roots) do
    if not roots[root] then
      vim.diagnostic.reset(sh.ns, nwbuf)
      pcall(vim.api.nvim_buf_delete, sh.buf, { force = true })
      st.roots[root] = nil
    end
  end
  local srclines = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(nwbuf, 0, -1, false)
  for root, info in pairs(roots) do
    local lines = tangle(nwbuf, root, info.cfg)
    if lines then
      local sh = ensure_shadow(nwbuf, root, info.lang, info.cfg)
      local content
      content, sh.src, sh.fwd, sh.marker =
        parse_maps(lines, info.cfg.leader, srclines)
      local old = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(sh.buf, 0, -1, false)
      if not vim.deep_equal(old, content) then
        -- the preview marks the shadow nomodifiable; lift it briefly
        local mod = vim.bo[sh.buf].modifiable
        vim.bo[sh.buf].modifiable = true
        vim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(sh.buf, 0, -1, false, content)
        vim.bo[sh.buf].modifiable = mod
      end
      forward_diagnostics(nwbuf, sh)
      local p = preview[nwbuf]
      if p and p.sh == sh and p.pbuf ~= sh.buf
          and vim.api.nvim_buf_is_valid(p.pbuf) then
        p.pv2t, p.t2pv = refresh_view(p.pbuf, sh)
      end
    end
  end
  return st
end
@

Queries need to know which shadow, if any, holds a given source
line, and where.  Prose lines are in no shadow; a line of a chunk
spliced several times maps to its first splice.

<<shadow: locate a source position in a shadow>>=
-- Locate source line lnum: returns shadow, tangled line, column delta.
function M.locate(nwbuf, lnum)
  nwbuf = (nwbuf and nwbuf ~= 0) and nwbuf or vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
  local st = M.sync(nwbuf)
  if not st then
    return nil
  end
  for _, sh in pairs(st.roots) do
    local t = sh.fwd[lnum]
    if t then
      return sh, t, col_delta(sh, t, nwbuf)
    end
  end
  return nil
end
@

Setup runs once per noweb buffer, from the ftplugin: an initial
sync, a debounced re-sync on edits (leaving insert mode or any
change in normal mode), and cleanup when the buffer goes away.  The
debounce keeps diagnostics live without tangling on every
keystroke; queries call [[M.sync]] themselves, so they never see a
stale shadow regardless of the timer.

<<shadow: setup>>=
function M.setup(nwbuf)
  nwbuf = (nwbuf and nwbuf ~= 0) and nwbuf or vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
  if state[nwbuf] then
    return
  end
  state[nwbuf] = { tick = -1, roots = {} }
  local group = vim.api.nvim_create_augroup('noweb-shadow:' .. nwbuf, {})
  local pending = false
  vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd({ 'TextChanged', 'InsertLeave' }, {
    group = group,
    buffer = nwbuf,
    callback = function()
      if not pending then
        pending = true
        vim.defer_fn(function()
          pending = false
          if vim.api.nvim_buf_is_valid(nwbuf) then
            M.sync(nwbuf)
          end
        end, 300)
      end
    end,
  })
  vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('BufWipeout', {
    group = group,
    buffer = nwbuf,
    callback = function()
      for _, sh in pairs(state[nwbuf].roots) do
        pcall(vim.api.nvim_buf_delete, sh.buf, { force = true })
      end
      state[nwbuf] = nil
      vim.api.nvim_del_augroup_by_id(group)
    end,
  })
  M.sync(nwbuf)
end
@

\subsection{Queries against the shadow}
\label{sec:shadowqueries}

The three interactive queries---completion, hover, definition---all
follow the same shape: map the cursor position into the shadow
(line through [[fwd]], column shifted by the splice indent), send
the LSP request \emph{synchronously} against the shadow buffer, and
map the answer back.  Synchronous is the right call here: an
omnifunc must return its candidates in-call, and a warm language
server answers in milliseconds.  (Positions use byte columns; LSP
counts UTF-16 units, so heavily non-ASCII lines can be off within
the line---a documented approximation, not worth the encoding dance
until it bites.)

Completion converts LSP items to omnifunc dictionaries---the
inserted [[word]], the displayed [[abbr]], the item kind in
[[menu]], documentation in [[info]]---and filters by the typed
prefix, since a server returns everything visible at the position.

<<shadow: LSP queries at the cursor>>=
local function cursor_params(nwbuf)
  local lnum, col = unpack(vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0))
  local sh, t, delta = M.locate(nwbuf, lnum)
  if not sh or #vim.lsp.get_clients({ bufnr = sh.buf }) == 0 then
    return nil
  end
  return sh, {
    textDocument = { uri = vim.uri_from_bufnr(sh.buf) },
    position = { line = t - 1, character = col + delta },
  }
end

function M.complete(base)
  local nwbuf = vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
  local sh, params = cursor_params(nwbuf)
  if not sh then
    return {}
  end
  local results = vim.lsp.buf_request_sync(
    sh.buf, 'textDocument/completion', params, 3000)
  local items = {}
  for _, res in pairs(results or {}) do
    for _, it in ipairs((res.result and (res.result.items or res.result)) or {}) do
      -- Servers may offer snippet insertText ($0 placeholders); an
      -- omnifunc inserts plain text, so those fall back to the label.
      local word = it.label
      if (it.insertTextFormat or 1) == 1 then
        word = (it.textEdit and it.textEdit.newText)
          or it.insertText or it.label
      end
      if vim.startswith(it.filterText or it.label, base)
          or vim.startswith(word, base) then
        local doc = it.documentation
        table.insert(items, {
          word = word,
          abbr = it.label,
          menu = vim.lsp.protocol.CompletionItemKind[it.kind] or '',
          info = type(doc) == 'table' and doc.value or doc or '',
          icase = 0,
          dup = 0,
        })
      end
    end
  end
  return items
end
@

Hover shows the server's documentation in a floating window over
the [[.nw]] buffer; a [[false]] return lets the caller fall back to
the ordinary [[K]].  Definition results need one more map: a target
inside the shadow is translated back to its source line (and its
own column correction---the definition may sit at another splice
depth); a target in some other file is a real file, jumped to
directly.  Both jumps set the jump mark, so [[CTRL-O]] returns.

Not every language has a server, and no server is needed for the
common case: the shadow holds the \emph{whole} tangled program, so
when the LSP route yields nothing, a definition-pattern search over
the shadow finds the identifier's defining line---[[assignments() {]]
in a shell program, [[def f(]] in Python, a make target, and for
variables the declaration or first assignment ([[local]],
[[export]], [[x=...]], a [[for]] binding; for shell even a mid-line
assignment, so a variable born inside generated code is
found)---wherever the defining chunk sits, and the source map turns
the hit into a [[.nw]] jump.  The patterns live in the language configuration
([[definitions]], with [[@NAME@]] standing for the identifier), so
[[g:noweb_languages]] tunes them per language.  The fallback is
first-match with no notion of scope; that is what it costs to work
with no server at all, and the LSP answer wins whenever there is
one.

<<shadow: LSP queries at the cursor>>=
function M.hover()
  local sh, params = cursor_params(vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf())
  if not sh then
    return false
  end
  local results = vim.lsp.buf_request_sync(
    sh.buf, 'textDocument/hover', params, 3000)
  for _, res in pairs(results or {}) do
    if res.result and res.result.contents then
      local lines = vim.lsp.util.convert_input_to_markdown_lines(
        res.result.contents)
      if #lines > 0 then
        vim.lsp.util.open_floating_preview(lines, 'markdown',
          { border = 'rounded' })
        return true
      end
    end
  end
  return false
end

function M.definition()
  local nwbuf = vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
  local sh, params = cursor_params(nwbuf)
  if sh then
    local results = vim.lsp.buf_request_sync(
      sh.buf, 'textDocument/definition', params, 3000)
    for _, res in pairs(results or {}) do
      local locs = res.result or {}
      if locs.uri or locs.targetUri then
        locs = { locs }
      end
      for _, loc in ipairs(locs) do
        local uri = loc.uri or loc.targetUri
        local range = loc.range or loc.targetSelectionRange
        vim.cmd([[normal! m']])
        if uri == vim.uri_from_bufnr(sh.buf) then
          local nl = sh.src[range.start.line + 1]
          if nl then
            local d = col_delta(sh, range.start.line + 1, nwbuf)
            vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0,
              { nl, math.max(0, range.start.character - d) })
            return true
          end
        else
          local client = vim.lsp.get_clients({ bufnr = sh.buf })[1]
          vim.lsp.util.show_document(loc, client.offset_encoding,
            { focus = true })
          return true
        end
      end
    end
  end
  return M.pattern_definition(nwbuf)
end

-- The serverless fallback: search the shadow for a line matching one
-- of the language's definition patterns for the identifier under the
-- cursor, and jump to its source line in the .nw.
function M.pattern_definition(nwbuf)
  local lnum = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)[1]
  local sh = M.locate(nwbuf, lnum)
  if not sh or not sh.cfg.definitions then
    return false
  end
  local name = vim.fn.expand('<cword>')
  if name == '' then
    return false
  end
  -- pesc's % escapes are themselves magic in a gsub replacement
  local escaped = vim.pesc(name):gsub('%%', '%%%%')
  local pats = {}
  for _, p in ipairs(sh.cfg.definitions) do
    table.insert(pats, (p:gsub('@NAME@', escaped)))
  end
  local lines = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(sh.buf, 0, -1, false)
  for t, line in ipairs(lines) do
    if not sh.marker[t] and sh.src[t] then
      for _, pat in ipairs(pats) do
        local s = line:find(pat)
        if s then
          vim.cmd([[normal! m']])
          local d = col_delta(sh, t, nwbuf)
          vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0,
            { sh.src[t], math.max(0, s - 1 - d) })
          return true
        end
      end
    end
  end
  return false
end
@

\subsection{Routing the omnifunc}
\label{sec:router}

One omnifunc serves three targets, which keeps every completion
frontend working through the single interface it already knows.
The route is decided at [[findstart]] time and remembered for the
candidates call:

\begin{enumerate}
\item after an unclosed [[@<<]]: chunk names (\cref{sec:complete});
\item on a line some shadow holds: that shadow's language server;
\item in prose with VimTeX initialized: VimTeX's own omnifunc,
  delegated wholesale (it knows where a [[\cite]] or [[\ref]]
  argument begins).
\end{enumerate}

For the language-server route the start-of-word rule is the
keyword-character run before the cursor---what an identifier means
in most languages; the server itself decides what the candidates
are.

The chosen route is script state between the omnifunc's two calls.
It is initialized at load time so that a candidates call without a
preceding [[findstart]]---no real frontend does that, but a
re-sourced script would---degrades to an empty answer instead of an
error.

<<the route between the omnifunc's two calls>>=
let s:route = ''
@

<<route the completion and find its start>>=
let l:before = strpart(getline('.'), 0, col('.') - 1)
let l:start = matchend(l:before, '.*@\@1<!@<<')
if l:start >= 0 && stridx(l:before, '>', l:start) < 0
  let s:route = 'chunk'
  return l:start
endif
if has('nvim') && get(g:, 'noweb_shadow', 1)
      \ && luaeval('require("noweb.shadow").locate(0, _A) ~= nil', line('.'))
  let s:route = 'lsp'
  return col('.') - 1 - len(matchstr(l:before, '\k*$'))
endif
if exists('b:vimtex')
  let s:route = 'tex'
  return vimtex#complete#omnifunc(a:findstart, a:base)
endif
let s:route = ''
return -3
@

<<produce the candidates for the routed target>>=
if s:route ==# 'lsp'
  return luaeval('require("noweb.shadow").complete(_A)', a:base)
elseif s:route ==# 'tex'
  return vimtex#complete#omnifunc(a:findstart, a:base)
elseif s:route !=# 'chunk'
  return []
endif
<<collect the matching chunk names>>
@

\subsection{Hover and definitions}
\label{sec:hoverdef}

[[K]] and [[gd]] get shadow-aware variants that fall back to the
built-in behaviour ([['keywordprg']] and the local-declaration
search) whenever the cursor is outside every shadow or the server
has nothing to say---so the maps are safe buffer-wide, prose
included.  Chunk references keep their own goto: \texttt{CTRL-]}
through the tagfunc (\cref{sec:tagfunc}) is untouched.

<<[[noweb#hover]] and [[noweb#definition]]>>=
" K / gd with shadow-LSP answers inside code chunks, falling back to
" the built-in behaviour elsewhere.
function! noweb#hover() abort
  if !s:shadowed() || !luaeval('require("noweb.shadow").hover()')
    normal! K
  endif
endfunction

function! noweb#definition() abort
  if !s:shadowed() || !luaeval('require("noweb.shadow").definition()')
    normal! gd
  endif
endfunction

function! s:shadowed() abort
  return has('nvim') && get(g:, 'noweb_shadow', 1)
        \ && luaeval('require("noweb.shadow").locate(0, _A) ~= nil', line('.'))
endfunction
@

<<the hover and definition maps>>=
nnoremap <silent> <Plug>(noweb-hover) :<C-u>call noweb#hover()<CR>
nnoremap <silent> <Plug>(noweb-definition) :<C-u>call noweb#definition()<CR>
if !get(g:, 'noweb_no_maps', 0)
  if !hasmapto('<Plug>(noweb-hover)', 'n')
    nmap <buffer> K <Plug>(noweb-hover)
  endif
  if !hasmapto('<Plug>(noweb-definition)', 'n')
    nmap <buffer> gd <Plug>(noweb-definition)
  endif
endif
let b:undo_ftplugin .= ' | silent! nunmap <buffer> K'
      \ . ' | silent! nunmap <buffer> gd'
@

\subsection{Indenting through the shadow}
\label{sec:shadowindent}

Emacs's [[M-n q]] on a code chunk runs [[indent-region]] in the
chunk's major mode.  We have no major mode to run it in
(\cref{sec:chunkopts}), and \emph{that} is what the shadow is for: it
is a buffer of the right filetype, with the language's own indent
script attached, holding this very code.  So [[:NowebFillChunk]]
(\cref{sec:fillchunk}) indents there and carries the answer back.

Only the answer, and only the part of it that is indentation.  The
shadow's text is the \emph{tangled} code---chunk uses expanded, marker
comments interleaved---so its lines are not the source's lines and
must not be copied over them.  What transfers is the \emph{change} in
each line's leading whitespace, added to what the source line had.
Each line's splice offset (how much deeper the tangle sits than the
source, \cref{sec:shadow}) is measured before the indent and
subtracted after, so a chunk keeps its own left margin no matter how
deeply it happens to be spliced.

This also decides what to indent \emph{with}.  A language server's
range formatting would be the fancier answer and is the wrong one: it
may insert or delete lines, and every line map here is an index into a
fixed set of lines.  Vim's own [[=]] cannot change the line count, so
the maps stay valid across the operation---the same reason Emacs
reaches for [[indent-region]] rather than a reformatter.

<<shadow: indent a source range through the shadow>>=
-- Re-indent .nw lines first..last using the shadow's own indenter.
-- Only the change in indentation crosses back: the shadow's text is
-- the tangled code, not the source's.  Returns false when no shadow
-- covers the range, so the caller can fall back to plain '='.
function M.indent(nwbuf, first, last)
  nwbuf = (nwbuf and nwbuf ~= 0) and nwbuf or vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
  local st = M.sync(nwbuf)
  if not st then
    return false
  end
  local sh
  for _, s in pairs(st.roots) do
    if s.fwd[first] then
      sh = s
      break
    end
  end
  if not sh or not vim.api.nvim_buf_is_valid(sh.buf) then
    return false
  end
  <<shadow: measure the splice offsets>>
  <<shadow: run the language's indenter on the shadow>>
  <<shadow: carry the new indentation back>>
  return true
end
@

Everywhere else in this module, whitespace can be counted in
characters: the tangle reproduces the source's indentation verbatim,
so both sides spell it the same way and [[indent_width]] comparing
character counts is exact.  Re-indenting breaks that assumption---the
indenter writes whatever the shadow's own options say, which need not
be what the source used---so here, and only here, indentation is
measured in display columns.

<<shadow: measure indentation in columns>>=
-- Leading whitespace of a line in display columns.  indent_width
-- counts characters, which is exact everywhere the tangle copies the
-- source's whitespace verbatim; an indenter does not.
local function indent_columns(line, ts)
  local w = 0
  for c in (line:match('^[ \t]*') or ''):gmatch('.') do
    w = (c == '\t') and (w + ts - (w % ts)) or (w + 1)
  end
  return w
end
@

A line the map does not reach---a blank line notangle dropped, or one
inside a chunk this root does not use---makes the whole range
unmappable, and the caller falls back rather than indenting half of
it.

<<shadow: measure the splice offsets>>=
local ts = vim.bo[nwbuf].tabstop
local nw = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(nwbuf, first - 1, last, false)
local tline, splice = {}, {}
local tfirst, tlast
for i, line in ipairs(nw) do
  local t = sh.fwd[first + i - 1]
  if not t then
    return false
  end
  local cur = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(sh.buf, t - 1, t, false)[1] or ''
  tline[i] = t
  splice[i] = indent_columns(cur, ts) - indent_columns(line, ts)
  tfirst = (not tfirst or t < tfirst) and t or tfirst
  tlast = (not tlast or t > tlast) and t or tlast
end
@

The shadow is [[nomodifiable]] so that nobody edits a buffer whose
contents are about to be overwritten by the next sync; indenting it is
exactly the case where we do mean to, briefly.  Whatever the indenter
leaves behind is transient anyway---the next [[M.sync]] re-tangles the
whole shadow from the [[.nw]].

It indents with the [[.nw]] buffer's conventions, not its own.  A
shadow is created by [[ensure_shadow]] with nothing said about
[['shiftwidth']] or [['expandtab']], so left alone it would indent a
two-space Lua program in eight-column tabs---and the answer that came
back would be measured against a source that uses neither.

<<shadow: run the language's indenter on the shadow>>=
for _, opt in ipairs({ 'expandtab', 'shiftwidth', 'tabstop', 'softtabstop' }) do
  vim.bo[sh.buf][opt] = vim.bo[nwbuf][opt]
end
vim.bo[sh.buf].modifiable = true
local ok = pcall(function()
  vim.api.nvim_buf_call(sh.buf, function()
    vim.cmd(('silent keepjumps normal! %dGV%dG='):format(tfirst, tlast))
  end)
end)
vim.bo[sh.buf].modifiable = false
if not ok then
  return false
end
@

Rebuilding the source line means keeping its text and replacing its
leading whitespace, in whatever the [[.nw]] buffer indents with---a
literate program's tangled output has to preserve tabs
(\cref{sec:building}), so guessing spaces would be a poor idea.  A
blank line stays blank rather than acquiring an indent of its own.

<<shadow: carry the new indentation back>>=
local expandtab = vim.bo[nwbuf].expandtab
local out = {}
for i, line in ipairs(nw) do
  local t = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(sh.buf, tline[i] - 1, tline[i], false)[1]
  local body = line:gsub('^[ \t]*', '')
  local want = math.max(0, indent_columns(t or '', ts) - splice[i])
  if body == '' then
    out[i] = ''
  elseif expandtab then
    out[i] = string.rep(' ', want) .. body
  else
    out[i] = string.rep('\t', math.floor(want / ts))
      .. string.rep(' ', want % ts) .. body
  end
end
vim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(nwbuf, first - 1, last, false, out)
@

<<[[s:shadow_indent]]>>=
" Re-indent through the tangled shadow, which has the language's own
" indent script; 0 when no shadow covers these lines and the caller
" should fall back to a plain '='.
function! s:shadow_indent(first, last) abort
  if !has('nvim') || !get(g:, 'noweb_shadow', 1)
    return 0
  endif
  return luaeval('require("noweb.shadow").indent(0, _A[1], _A[2])',
        \ [a:first, a:last])
endfunction
@

\subsection{VimTeX for the prose}
\label{sec:vimtexprose}

The prose of a noweb file \emph{is} a \TeX{} document, and VimTeX
is the \TeX{} toolchain of choice: motions, text objects, the table
of contents, delimiter matching, and the completion the router
delegates to.  VimTeX only initializes itself for the [[tex]]
filetype, but its entire per-buffer setup is one documented call
away.  After it, two buffer-local options are taken back: VimTeX
claims the omnifunc (ours routes to VimTeX's instead), and the
tagfunc stays with chunk navigation.  Both undo scripts are
preserved, chained.  [[g:noweb_vimtex]] opts out wholesale.

<<initialize VimTeX for the prose>>=
if get(g:, 'noweb_vimtex', 1) && !exists('b:vimtex')
      \ && !empty(globpath(&runtimepath, 'autoload/vimtex.vim'))
  let s:undo = b:undo_ftplugin
  unlet b:undo_ftplugin
  silent! call vimtex#init()
  let b:undo_ftplugin = s:undo
        \ . (empty(get(b:, 'undo_ftplugin', ''))
        \    ? '' : ' | ' . b:undo_ftplugin)
  setlocal omnifunc=noweb#complete
  if exists('+tagfunc')
    setlocal tagfunc=noweb#tagfunc
  endif
endif
@

\subsection{A tangled preview, in lockstep}
\label{sec:preview}

[[:NowebTangled]] shows the tangled program in a read-only split and
keeps the two windows in lockstep.  What it shows is the \emph{build
artifact}: the linemark markers are the machinery's bookkeeping, so
the preview filters them out---a derived buffer holding exactly what
[[notangle]] without the filter would produce, with the language's
own syntax highlighting.  ([[g:noweb_tangled_markers = 1]] shows the
raw shadow instead, markers and all.)  The filtering needs no second
tangle: the marker set is known, so one pass derives the view and
two small maps between view lines and tangled lines, and the
cursor-sync lookups compose through them.

Moving in the source moves the preview to the corresponding tangled
line (the first splice, when the chunk is used several times);
moving in the preview moves the source cursor through the reverse
map, which is total on code lines.  Prose lines belong to no shadow,
so the preview simply stays put while the cursor crosses them.

The split obeys the usual command modifiers: plain [[:NowebTangled]]
opens below ([[g:noweb_tangled_split]] changes that default), and
[[:vertical NowebTangled]], [[:botright NowebTangled]] and friends
do what they say---the modifiers, when given, replace the default
wholesale.

Two details keep the lockstep calm.  Cursor moves made with
[[nvim_win_set_cursor]] into a \emph{non-current} window do not fire
that window's [[CursorMoved]], so the two autocommands cannot
ping-pong; a [[syncing]] flag guards the residual case (the user
switching windows mid-update) anyway.  And the command toggles:
running it again, or closing the preview window, tears the
autocommands down.

Naming the root should be forgiving: root chunks are conventionally
named with [[[[...]]]] quoting, but nobody wants to type brackets on
a command line, so an argument matches the root name exactly, the
name with the quoting added, or the name with the quoting stripped
(the command also completes root names).  Without an argument the
command previews the root whose tangle holds the cursor line; a
cursor on a chunk \emph{header} is resolved through the chunk's
first body line, so \enquote{on a chunk} works the way it reads.
Failing all that, a single root is used, and with several the error
lists them.

<<shadow: the synced preview>>=
local preview = {}  -- preview[nwbuf] = { win, nwwin, sh, group }
local syncing = false

local function preview_close(nwbuf)
  local p = preview[nwbuf]
  if not p then
    return
  end
  preview[nwbuf] = nil
  pcall(vim.api.nvim_del_augroup_by_id, p.group)
  if vim.api.nvim_win_is_valid(p.win) then
    pcall(vim.api.nvim_win_close, p.win, true)
  end
  if p.pbuf ~= p.sh.buf and vim.api.nvim_buf_is_valid(p.pbuf) then
    pcall(vim.api.nvim_buf_delete, p.pbuf, { force = true })
  end
end

-- The marker-free view of a shadow: fill pbuf with the non-marker
-- lines and return the two composition maps (view line <-> tangled
-- line; a marker maps forward to the next kept line).
local function refresh_view(pbuf, sh)
  local lines = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(sh.buf, 0, -1, false)
  local view, pv2t, t2pv = {}, {}, {}
  for t, line in ipairs(lines) do
    if sh.marker[t] then
      t2pv[t] = #view + 1
    else
      table.insert(view, line)
      pv2t[#view] = t
      t2pv[t] = #view
    end
  end
  for t, pv in pairs(t2pv) do
    if pv > #view then
      t2pv[t] = #view
    end
  end
  vim.bo[pbuf].modifiable = true
  vim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(pbuf, 0, -1, false, view)
  vim.bo[pbuf].modifiable = false
  return pv2t, t2pv
end

local function strip_quotes(name)
  return (name:gsub('%[%[', ''):gsub('%]%]', ''))
end

local function preview_pick(st, root, lnum)
  if root and root ~= '' then
    if st.roots[root] then
      return st.roots[root]
    end
    for name, sh in pairs(st.roots) do
      if name == '[[' .. root .. ']]' or strip_quotes(name) == root then
        return sh
      end
    end
    return nil
  end
  local only, n, holder = nil, 0, nil
  for _, sh in pairs(st.roots) do
    n = n + 1
    only = sh
    if sh.fwd[lnum] or sh.fwd[lnum + 1] then
      holder = sh
    end
  end
  return holder or (n == 1 and only or nil)
end

-- Root names of the current buffer's shadows, for command completion.
function M.roots()
  local st = M.sync(vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf())
  local out = {}
  for name in pairs(st and st.roots or {}) do
    table.insert(out, name)
  end
  table.sort(out)
  return out
end

function M.preview(root, mods)
  local nwbuf = vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
  if preview[nwbuf] then
    preview_close(nwbuf)
    return
  end
  local st = M.sync(nwbuf)
  if not st then
    return
  end
  local lnum = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)[1]
  local sh = preview_pick(st, root, lnum)
  if not sh then
    vim.notify('noweb: name the root: :NowebTangled <root>  ('
      .. table.concat(M.roots(), ', ') .. ')', vim.log.levels.WARN)
    return
  end
  local show, pv2t, t2pv = sh.buf, nil, nil
  if vim.g.noweb_tangled_markers ~= 1 then
    show = vim.api.nvim_create_buf(false, true)
    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_name(show,
      shadow_name(nwbuf, sh.root) .. ' (tangled)')
    pv2t, t2pv = refresh_view(show, sh)
    vim.bo[show].filetype = sh.cfg.filetype
  else
    vim.bo[show].modifiable = false
  end
  local nwwin = vim.api.nvim_get_current_win()
  if mods and mods ~= '' then
    vim.cmd(mods .. ' split')
  else
    vim.cmd(vim.g.noweb_tangled_split or 'rightbelow split')
  end
  local pwin = vim.api.nvim_get_current_win()
  vim.api.nvim_win_set_buf(pwin, show)
  vim.wo[pwin].cursorline = true
  local group = vim.api.nvim_create_augroup('noweb-preview:' .. nwbuf, {})
  preview[nwbuf] = { win = pwin, nwwin = nwwin, sh = sh, group = group,
                     pbuf = show, pv2t = pv2t, t2pv = t2pv }
  <<shadow: the two cursor-sync autocommands>>
  local t = sh.fwd[lnum]
  if t then
    vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(pwin, { t2pv and t2pv[t] or t, 0 })
    vim.api.nvim_win_call(pwin, function() vim.cmd('normal! zz') end)
  end
  vim.api.nvim_set_current_win(nwwin)
end
@

<<shadow: the two cursor-sync autocommands>>=
vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('CursorMoved', {
  group = group,
  buffer = nwbuf,
  callback = function()
    local p = preview[nwbuf]
    if syncing or not p or not vim.api.nvim_win_is_valid(p.win) then
      return
    end
    local t = p.sh.fwd[vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)[1]]
    if t then
      syncing = true
      vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(p.win, { p.t2pv and p.t2pv[t] or t, 0 })
      vim.api.nvim_win_call(p.win, function() vim.cmd('normal! zz') end)
      syncing = false
    end
  end,
})
vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('CursorMoved', {
  group = group,
  buffer = show,
  callback = function()
    local p = preview[nwbuf]
    if syncing or not p or not vim.api.nvim_win_is_valid(p.nwwin) then
      return
    end
    local l = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)[1]
    local s = p.sh.src[p.pv2t and p.pv2t[l] or l]
    if s then
      syncing = true
      vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(p.nwwin, { s, 0 })
      vim.api.nvim_win_call(p.nwwin, function() vim.cmd('normal! zz') end)
      syncing = false
    end
  end,
})
vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('WinClosed', {
  group = group,
  pattern = tostring(pwin),
  callback = function()
    preview_close(nwbuf)
  end,
})
@

\subsection{Building through nolinemap}
\label{sec:nowebmake}

For everything a language server does not cover---running the
program, a linter, a type checker---noweb's \texttt{nolinemap}
already solves the mapping problem at the process level: it runs a
command and rewrites every file-and-line reference in its output to
point into the [[.nw]] source, columns included.  [[:NowebMake
python3 %]] tangles the root under the cursor to a temporary file
([[%]] stands for it), runs the command through nolinemap, and
loads the rewritten messages into the quickfix list---which then
jumps straight into the noweb source.

The tangle here reads the \emph{file}, not the buffer: the markers
must carry the real path for nolinemap's rewriting to name it, so
the command insists on a saved buffer, exactly like [[:make]].
The output gets the same repairs as the shadows---junctions
rejoined, continuation-severing markers dropped---so the compiler
sees the real program; the markers that remain are whole-line
comments nolinemap reads exactly as we do (a dropped marker costs
nolinemap some precision around that splice, the price of handing
it valid code).

<<shadow: quickfix through nolinemap>>=
function M.make(cmd)
  local nwbuf = vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
  if vim.bo[nwbuf].modified then
    vim.notify('noweb: save the buffer first; :NowebMake builds the file',
      vim.log.levels.WARN)
    return
  end
  local st = M.sync(nwbuf)
  local lnum = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)[1]
  local sh = st and preview_pick(st, nil, lnum)
  if not sh then
    vim.notify('noweb: no typed root here', vim.log.levels.WARN)
    return
  end
  local nwfile = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_name(nwbuf)
  local tmp = vim.fn.tempname() .. (sh.root:match('%.%w+$') or '')
  local tangle = vim.system({ 'sh', '-c', string.format(
    'notangle %s -filter "linemark %s-c \'%s\'" -R\'%s\' \'%s\' > \'%s\'',
    table.concat(sh.cfg.tangle or {}, ' '),
    eager_flag(), sh.cfg.leader, sh.root, nwfile, tmp) }):wait()
  if tangle.code ~= 0 and vim.fn.getfsize(tmp) <= 0 then
    vim.notify('noweb: tangling failed: ' .. (tangle.stderr or ''),
      vim.log.levels.ERROR)
    return
  end
  vim.fn.writefile(
    (parse_maps(rejoin_splices(vim.fn.readfile(tmp), sh.cfg.leader),
                sh.cfg.leader, {})), tmp)
  local run = vim.system(
    { 'sh', '-c', 'nolinemap ' .. cmd:gsub('%%', tmp) .. ' 2>&1' },
    { text = true }):wait()
  vim.fn.setqflist({}, ' ', {
    title = 'NowebMake: ' .. cmd,
    lines = vim.split(run.stdout or '', '\n'),
    efm = '%*[ ]File "%f"\\, line %l%.%#,'
      .. '%f:%l:%c: %m,%f:%l: %m,%-G%.%#',
  })
  vim.cmd('copen | wincmd p')
end
@

\subsection{Hooking the shadows into the buffer}

The ftplugin starts the machinery on Neovim unless the user opts
out; everything degrades gracefully when notangle (or linemark) is
missing---the module simply produces no shadows, and the plugin is
back to its v1.6.1 feature set.

<<start the shadow machinery>>=
if has('nvim') && get(g:, 'noweb_shadow', 1) && executable('notangle')
  lua require('noweb.shadow').setup(0)
  command! -buffer -nargs=? -complete=customlist,noweb#tangled_roots
        \ NowebTangled
        \ call luaeval('require("noweb.shadow").preview(_A[1], _A[2])',
        \              [<q-args>, <q-mods>])
  command! -buffer -nargs=+ NowebMake
        \ call luaeval('require("noweb.shadow").make(_A)', <q-args>)
  let b:undo_ftplugin .= ' | silent! delcommand -buffer NowebTangled'
        \ . ' | silent! delcommand -buffer NowebMake'
endif
@

\section{Editing in chunks}
\label{sec:editing}

Everything so far has been about \emph{understanding} the buffer: what
language a chunk is in, where a name is defined, what a language
server makes of the code.  This section is about \emph{editing} it,
and it closes the one gap where noweb's own Emacs mode has been ahead
of this plugin all along.

That mode, \texttt{noweb-mode.el}, is a minor \enquote{meta} mode:
after every command it looks at where point is and switches the
buffer's \emph{major} mode to [[noweb-code-mode]] or
[[noweb-doc-mode]], narrowing to the chunk first.  One trick, and
chunk-aware marking, killing, filling and indenting all fall out of
it, because they are the major mode's own commands operating on a
buffer that has been made to look like a single-language file.  The
price is steep---one code mode for the whole buffer, chosen by hand in
a modeline; one chunk fontified at a time; no notion of what language
anything is written in---which is why this plugin took the opposite
route from v1.3 on: the filetype stays [[noweb]] forever and
everything per-chunk is layered on top of it
(\cref{sec:refresh,sec:shadow}).

The layering wins what it set out to win.  What it never did was touch
the buffer's \emph{editing} options, and the omission shows.  In a
Python chunk [['commentstring']] is still \TeX{}'s, so a commenting
plugin inserts [[%]].  [[gq]] reflows prose straight through a chunk
header, and splits [[[[...]]]] quotes down the middle.  And there is
no way at all to say \enquote{this chunk} to an operator.  What
follows supplies that missing half---but in Vim's idiom rather than by
transliterating Emacs: text objects and motions instead of
mark/kill/copy commands, folds instead of narrowing, a
[['formatexpr']] instead of a replacement for [[do-auto-fill]].

\subsection{Where a chunk begins and ends}
\label{sec:bounds}

Every item in this section needs the same answer: which chunk is line
$n$ in, and where does it start and stop?  The occurrence scan of
\cref{sec:occurrences} walks the whole buffer to answer a global
question; this is a local one, asked on every cursor movement
(\cref{sec:chunkopts}), so it must cost two searches rather than a
pass.

Three patterns, not one, because noweb's [[@ %def]] line is genuinely
ambiguous.  It is an index declaration---\enquote{this chunk defines
these identifiers}---and it matches [[^@ ]], so it \emph{does} end the
code chunk: a [[@<<name@>>]] written after it is prose, not a chunk
use, and the scans of \cref{sec:infer,sec:occurrences} are right to
leave code mode there.  But it plainly belongs to the chunk above it,
and Emacs agrees: [[noweb-update-chunk-vector]] skips a run of
[[@ %def]] lines before deciding what the next chunk is, and
[[noweb-fill-chunk]] excludes them from the region it fills.  So a
chunk gets two ends---the code proper, and the code plus its
declarations---and the callers pick.

<<the chunk-boundary patterns>>=
" Where chunks begin and end.  s:DEFLINE starts a code chunk,
" s:TEXTLINE a documentation chunk; together (s:HEAD) they are every
" line a chunk can start on.  s:PCTDEF is noweb's index declaration:
" it ends the code but belongs to the chunk, so s:BOUND -- where a
" code body must stop -- includes it while s:HEAD does not.
let s:DEFLINE = '^@<<.\{-1,}@>>=\s*$'
let s:PCTDEF = '^@ %def\%( \|$\)'
let s:TEXTLINE = '^@\%( %def\)\@!\%( \|$\)'
let s:HEAD = s:DEFLINE . '\|' . s:TEXTLINE
let s:BOUND = s:HEAD . '\|' . s:PCTDEF
@

The head is the nearest [[s:HEAD]] line at or above [[lnum]]---zero
when the file opens with prose before any marker, which is legal and
common.  From the head, one forward search for [[s:HEAD]] finds where
the next chunk starts, and one for [[s:BOUND]] finds where this
chunk's code stops; between the two lie the [[@ %def]] declarations,
if any.  Both searches run with the cursor parked on the head and the
[[n]] flag set, so neither disturbs the other, and the view is saved
around the whole thing.

<<[[s:chunk_bounds]]>>=
" The chunk containing line lnum, as
"   kind  'code' or 'doc'
"   name  the chunk's name; '' for a documentation chunk
"   head  its @<<name@>>= or @ line; 0 before the file's first marker
"   body  its first line of content
"   last  its last line of content
"   end   its last line, "@ %def" declarations included
" 'last' and 'end' differ only for a code chunk with declarations:
" 'ic' stops at 'last', 'ac' runs to 'end'.
function! s:chunk_bounds(lnum) abort
  <<answer from the memo when the cursor has not left the chunk>>
  let l:view = winsaveview()
  try
    call cursor(a:lnum, 1)
    let l:head = search(s:HEAD, 'bcnW')
    call cursor(max([l:head, 1]), 1)
    let l:next = search(s:HEAD, 'nW')
    let l:stop = search(s:BOUND, 'nW')
  finally
    call winrestview(l:view)
  endtry
  <<name the chunk and place its body>>
  <<remember the bounds and return them>>
endfunction
@

A documentation chunk's [[@]] line is markup when it is bare and prose
when anything follows it---[[@ So much for the parser.]] is a first
line of text, and an operator on the chunk's body should include it.
A code chunk's header line is always markup.

<<name the chunk and place its body>>=
let l:code = l:head > 0 && getline(l:head) =~# s:DEFLINE
let l:end = l:next > 0 ? l:next - 1 : line('$')
let l:bounds = {
      \ 'kind': l:code ? 'code' : 'doc',
      \ 'name': l:code ? matchstr(getline(l:head), s:DEF) : '',
      \ 'head': l:head,
      \ 'body': l:head == 0 || (!l:code && getline(l:head) !~# '^@\s*$')
      \         ? max([l:head, 1]) : l:head + 1,
      \ 'last': l:stop > 0 ? min([l:stop - 1, l:end]) : l:end,
      \ 'end': l:end,
      \ }
@

The memo is one entry, not a dictionary of them: the caller that makes
this hot is the cursor-movement hook, and it asks about the same chunk
again and again until the cursor leaves it.  [[b:changedtick]] guards
against an edit having moved the boundaries, exactly as it does for
the occurrence scan (\cref{sec:occurrences}).

<<answer from the memo when the cursor has not left the chunk>>=
if get(s:memo, 'buf', -1) == bufnr('%') && s:memo.tick == b:changedtick
      \ && a:lnum >= max([s:memo.bounds.head, 1])
      \ && a:lnum <= s:memo.bounds.end
  return s:memo.bounds
endif
@

<<remember the bounds and return them>>=
let s:memo = {'buf': bufnr('%'), 'tick': b:changedtick, 'bounds': l:bounds}
return l:bounds
@

<<the chunk-bounds memo>>=
" One-entry memo for s:chunk_bounds; see s:chunk_options.
let s:memo = {}
@

\subsection{Chunk text objects}
\label{sec:textobj}

Emacs spends six commands and six key bindings on marking, killing and
copying a chunk and a chunk \emph{pair}
([[noweb-mark-chunk]], [[-kill-chunk]], [[-copy-chunk-as-kill]] and
their uppercase pair variants).  Vim spends none: a text object is not
an operation, it is an \emph{argument} to every operation the editor
has, so [[dac]], [[yic]] and [[vaC]] cover all six, and [[gqic]],
[[=ic]] and [[gcic]] come along uncounted.  This is the clearest case
in the whole plugin where the Vim idiom is smaller \emph{and} does
more.

The objects are linewise.  A chunk is a run of whole lines by
construction---its delimiters occupy the first column---and every
operator worth applying to one wants it that way.

\begin{description}
\item[[[ic]]] the chunk's content: the body without the header or the
  [[@ %def]] tail.
\item[[[ac]]] the whole chunk, markup included.
\item[[[iC]], [[aC]]] the same for the documentation/code
  \emph{pair}, noweb's real unit of composition---a paragraph of prose
  and the code it introduces.  This is Emacs's
  [[noweb-chunk-pair-region]]: from a code chunk it reaches back over
  the prose that introduces it, and from a documentation chunk it
  reaches forward over the code that follows.
\end{description}

<<[[noweb#textobj]]>>=
" The ic / ac / iC / aC text objects.  Linewise, and defined for both
" operator-pending and visual mode by the same function.
function! noweb#textobj(pair, around) abort
  let l:c = s:chunk_bounds(line('.'))
  if a:pair
    let l:c = s:pair_bounds(l:c)
  endif
  let l:first = a:around ? max([l:c.head, 1]) : l:c.body
  let l:last = a:around ? l:c.end : l:c.last
  if l:last < l:first
    " An empty chunk has no inside to select.
    return
  endif
  call cursor(l:first, 1)
  normal! V
  call cursor(l:last, 1)
endfunction
@

Reaching for the other half of the pair is a chunk-bounds query one
line outside the current chunk---and \enquote{the code that follows}
may be several documentation chunks away, so that direction loops.  It
stops at the end of the file rather than running off it: an unpaired
trailing documentation chunk is just itself.

<<[[s:pair_bounds]]>>=
" Extend a chunk to its documentation/code pair: back over the prose
" that introduces a code chunk, or forward to the code a documentation
" chunk introduces (Emacs's noweb-chunk-pair-region).
function! s:pair_bounds(c) abort
  if a:c.kind ==# 'code'
    if a:c.head <= 1
      return a:c
    endif
    let l:doc = s:chunk_bounds(a:c.head - 1)
    return extend(copy(a:c), {'head': l:doc.head, 'body': l:doc.body})
  endif
  let l:code = a:c
  while l:code.kind !=# 'code' && l:code.end < line('$')
    let l:code = s:chunk_bounds(l:code.end + 1)
  endwhile
  return extend(copy(a:c), {'last': l:code.last, 'end': l:code.end})
endfunction
@

\subsection{Chunk motions}
\label{sec:chunkmotions}

\texttt{]c} and \texttt{[c} (\cref{sec:refs}) step through the
occurrences of one \emph{name}.  What Emacs also has, and this plugin
did not, is plain structural movement: next chunk, next code chunk,
next documentation chunk, and their reverses---six commands under
\texttt{M-n}.  In Vim they are bracket motions, and the family already
has conventional members to borrow: \texttt{]{]}} and
\texttt{[{[}} move by section, \texttt{]m} and \texttt{[m} by
\enquote{method}, and \texttt{][} and \texttt{[]} to a section's
other end.  A noweb source has no C sections or methods for those keys
to mean anything else, so:

\begin{description}
\item[Either kind:] \texttt{]{]}} and \texttt{[{[}}.
\item[Code chunks:] \texttt{]m} and \texttt{[m}---the useful one, and
  the reason \texttt{]m} rather than a second section motion.
\item[Documentation chunks:] \texttt{][} and \texttt{[]}.
\end{description}

(The braces in \texttt{]{]}} are for the weave, not the reader: a
literal \texttt{[}\texttt{[} in prose would open a noweb code quote
that runs until the next \texttt{]}\texttt{]}, and a \texttt{]}
inside an \texttt{\char`\\item} label would end the label.  This
document's own conventions, \cref{sec:building}, cover the angle
brackets; the square ones bite exactly once, here.)

They take a count, they wrap, and they set the [['']] mark first, so
\texttt{CTRL-O} comes back---all three exactly as
[[noweb#next_occurrence]] (\cref{sec:refs}) does, because a motion
that behaves differently from its neighbour is worse than no motion.
Wrapping is where we part company with Emacs, whose TODO list still
asks for it.

<<[[noweb#next_chunk]]>>=
" The ]] / [[ / ]m / [m / ][ / [] motions: the count'th next (dir > 0)
" or previous chunk of the given kind, wrapping.
function! noweb#next_chunk(dir, kind, count) abort
  let l:pat = a:kind ==# 'code' ? s:DEFLINE
        \ : a:kind ==# 'doc' ? s:TEXTLINE : s:HEAD
  normal! m'
  for l:i in range(max([a:count, 1]))
    call search(l:pat, a:dir > 0 ? 'w' : 'bw')
  endfor
endfunction
@

\subsection{Filling prose that has code in it}
\label{sec:format}

[[gq]] in a noweb source is wrong twice over.  It reflows straight
through a chunk header, because [[@<<name@>>=]] is not a paragraph
break to Vim; and it breaks lines inside [[[[...]]]] quotes, turning
one code fragment into two and stranding a [[]]]] at the start of a
line.  Emacs has had an answer since v2.11:
[[noweb-fill-chunk]] narrows to the chunk before filling, and
[[noweb-hide-code-quotes]] blanks out the quotes' contents so the
filler cannot misread code punctuation as prose.

Vim's hook for this is [['formatexpr']], which every formatting
command routes through---[[gq]], [[gw]], and the automatic wrapping
that [['formatoptions']] drives.  It is handed a range in [[v:lnum]]
and [[v:count]] and returns non-zero to mean \enquote{never mind, do
it the built-in way}.

Two departures from Emacs are worth naming.  First, a range spanning
several chunks is not clamped to the first one and quietly truncated;
it is \emph{split}, and each piece formatted within its own chunk, so
[[gqG]] does the whole rest of the file without ever joining a
paragraph to a chunk header.  The pieces are formatted last-first, so
that reflowing one does not shift the line numbers of those still to
come.  Second, where Emacs merely stops the filler from misreading a
quote, we stop it from \emph{breaking} one: a [[[[...]]]] quote is a
single conceptual token, and a user who wrote one wants it whole.  The
stronger guarantee subsumes the weaker one and is easier to state.

<<[[noweb#format]]>>=
" 'formatexpr': fill v:lnum..v:lnum+v:count-1, chunk by chunk.
function! noweb#format() abort
  if v:char !=# ''
    " Auto-wrapping as you type, one character at a time; the built-in
    " formatter already stops at the chunk's blank lines.
    return 1
  endif
  let l:last = v:lnum + v:count - 1
  <<split the range into chunk pieces>>
  <<spare the code when the range is a sweep>>
  for l:piece in l:pieces
    call s:format_piece(l:piece)
  endfor
  return 0
endfunction
@

A range covering more than one chunk is a \emph{sweep}---[[gqG]],
[[gqac]] over a pair, [[gggqG]] on the whole file---and what the user
means by it is \enquote{tidy the prose}.  Reflowing code as if it were
prose would join a function body into one line, which is what plain
[[gq]] in a [[.nw]] buffer used to do and nobody ever wanted.  A range
that lies inside a single chunk, on the other hand, is deliberate:
[[gqip]] over a run of comments inside a code chunk is the one case
where filling code is exactly right, and with [['comments']] set to
the chunk language's own (\cref{sec:chunkopts}) it rewraps them with
their leaders intact.  So: one piece, format it whatever it is; more
than one, format only the prose.

<<spare the code when the range is a sweep>>=
if len(l:pieces) > 1
  call filter(l:pieces, {_, p -> p.kind ==# 'doc'})
endif
@

Each step of the walk asks for the chunk at the current line, takes the
part of it that lies inside the requested range, and resumes past the
chunk's end---past [[end]] rather than [[last]], so a code chunk's
[[@ %def]] declarations are stepped over rather than filled as prose.

<<split the range into chunk pieces>>=
let l:pieces = []
let l:n = v:lnum
while l:n <= l:last
  let l:c = s:chunk_bounds(l:n)
  " Never below the chunk's body: a range that starts on a header line
  " must not invite the formatter to join the header to the code.
  let l:from = max([l:n, l:c.body])
  let l:to = min([l:c.last, l:last])
  if l:to >= l:from
    call add(l:pieces, {'kind': l:c.kind, 'from': l:from, 'to': l:to})
  endif
  let l:n = max([l:c.end, l:n]) + 1
endwhile
" Later pieces first: filling one piece moves everything below it.
call reverse(l:pieces)
@

Code is handed to the built-in formatter unchanged.  That is not a
concession: by the time it runs, [['comments']] and
[['formatoptions']] are the chunk language's own
(\cref{sec:chunkopts}), so [[gq]] on a run of Python comments rewraps
them with [[#]] leaders, which is exactly what Emacs's mode switch
buys and all anyone wants here.

<<[[s:format_piece]]>>=
" Fill one piece of one chunk.  Each step can change how many lines
" the piece occupies, so its end is carried along rather than reread.
function! s:format_piece(p) abort
  if a:p.kind ==# 'code'
    call s:internal_format(a:p.from, a:p.to)
    return
  endif
  let l:to = a:p.to
  <<rejoin code quotes split across lines>>
  <<hide the spaces inside the code quotes>>
  let l:before = line('$')
  call s:internal_format(a:p.from, l:to)
  let l:to += line('$') - l:before
  <<put the spaces back>>
endfunction
@

A quote that is \emph{already} broken across two lines defeats the
protection below before it starts: neither line holds a whole
[[[[...]]]], so neither is recognized, and the fill is free to join
the two and break them somewhere else.  Real documents are full of
these---this one has four---because a quote is only ever split by a
previous fill.  Joining them first makes them visible, and costs
nothing that was not going to happen: filling a paragraph joins its
lines anyway.  Only within a paragraph, though; a blank line ends the
search, or an unterminated quote would swallow the rest of the chunk.

<<rejoin code quotes split across lines>>=
let l:n = a:p.from
while l:n < l:to
  if s:quote_open(getline(l:n)) && getline(l:n + 1) !~# '^\s*$'
    call setline(l:n, getline(l:n) . ' '
          \ . substitute(getline(l:n + 1), '^\s*', '', ''))
    execute 'silent ' . (l:n + 1) . 'delete _'
    let l:to -= 1
  else
    let l:n += 1
  endif
endwhile
@

Deciding whether a line leaves a quote open is a counting question,
and the naive test---\enquote{is there a [[[[]] after the last
[[]]]]?}---gets this document's own convention wrong, since a literal
[[[[]] is written [[[[[[]]]] and a literal [[]]]] as [[]]]]]].
Striking out the complete quotes first and asking what is left over
handles both.

<<[[s:quote_open]]>>=
" Does the line leave a [[...]] quote unclosed?  Complete quotes are
" struck out first, so the doubled brackets that write a literal
" bracket do not read as an opening one.
function! s:quote_open(line) abort
  return substitute(a:line, s:QUOTE, '', 'g') =~# '\[\['
endfunction
@

The trick for keeping a quote whole is to take away the only thing the
formatter can break at.  Replacing the blanks \emph{inside}
[[[[...]]]] with a character that occurs nowhere in the text makes
each quote a single unbreakable word for the duration of the fill;
afterwards the blanks go back.

\enquote{Nowhere in the text} has to mean nowhere in the \emph{piece},
not nowhere in the buffer, and the difference is not academic.  The
stand-ins are private-use codepoints, chosen because documents do not
contain them---except that this one does, all three of them, on the
line below that names them: a literate program prints its own source.
Searching the whole buffer therefore found every candidate in use and
silently turned the protection off, for exactly the document most
likely to be tested with.  Only the lines being substituted into and
read back out of can be affected, so only those need checking---which
also spares a buffer-wide scan per piece.

<<the code-quote pattern and its stand-in>>=
" A [[...]] quote, and the private-use characters we borrow to stand
" in for the blanks inside one while the formatter runs.
let s:QUOTE = '\[\[.\{-}\]\]'
let s:FILLERS = [nr2char(0xe000), nr2char(0xe001), nr2char(0xe002)]
@

<<[[s:free_filler]]>>=
" A stand-in appearing in none of these lines, or '' if every
" candidate somehow does -- then the fill still happens, just without
" the protection.
function! s:free_filler(lines) abort
  for l:c in s:FILLERS
    if match(a:lines, l:c) < 0
      return l:c
    endif
  endfor
  return ''
endfunction
@

<<hide the spaces inside the code quotes>>=
let l:lines = getline(a:p.from, l:to)
let l:filler = s:free_filler(l:lines)
if l:filler !=# ''
  let l:hidden = []
  for l:line in l:lines
    call add(l:hidden, s:hide_quotes(l:line, l:filler))
  endfor
  call setline(a:p.from, l:hidden)
endif
@

Walking the line by hand rather than reaching for a [[\=]] replacement
expression is not squeamishness: the substitution has to look inside
its own match, and a nested [[substitute()]] under [[\=]] inside a
lambda has neither the submatch nor the enclosing local in the scope
one would expect.

<<[[s:hide_quotes]]>>=
" Replace the blanks inside every [[...]] quote on the line with the
" stand-in, leaving each quote a single unbreakable word.
function! s:hide_quotes(line, filler) abort
  let l:out = ''
  let l:rest = a:line
  while 1
    let l:mp = matchstrpos(l:rest, s:QUOTE)
    if l:mp[1] < 0
      return l:out . l:rest
    endif
    let l:out .= strpart(l:rest, 0, l:mp[1])
          \ . substitute(l:mp[0], '[ \t]', a:filler, 'g')
    let l:rest = strpart(l:rest, l:mp[2])
  endwhile
endfunction
@

<<put the spaces back>>=
if l:filler !=# ''
  let l:shown = []
  for l:line in getline(a:p.from, l:to)
    call add(l:shown, substitute(l:line, l:filler, ' ', 'g'))
  endfor
  call setline(a:p.from, l:shown)
endif
@

Running the built-in formatter from inside [['formatexpr']] means
taking [['formatexpr']] out of the way first---it is us, and we would
recurse.  A linewise visual selection is the shortest way to hand
[[gq]] an exact range, and folding has to come off around it: a
linewise operator takes a closed fold as a whole
(\texttt{:help fold-behavior}), so with \cref{sec:folding} turned on
the fill of one chunk would reach into its neighbours.  These ranges
are exact by construction and must be honoured exactly.

<<[[s:internal_format]]>>=
" Vim's own formatter over first..last, with 'formatexpr' (this file)
" unset so that gq does not call us back.
function! s:internal_format(first, last) abort
  let l:save = &l:formatexpr
  try
    let &l:formatexpr = ''
    call s:normal_over(a:first, a:last, 'gq')
  finally
    let &l:formatexpr = l:save
  endtry
endfunction

" Apply a linewise operator to exactly first..last, with folding off:
" a closed fold would extend the operator over the whole fold.
function! s:normal_over(first, last, op) abort
  let l:foldenable = &l:foldenable
  try
    setlocal nofoldenable
    call cursor(a:first, 1)
    execute 'normal! V'
          \ . (a:last > a:first ? (a:last - a:first) . 'j' : '') . a:op
  finally
    let &l:foldenable = l:foldenable
  endtry
endfunction
@

\subsection{Formatting a whole chunk}
\label{sec:fillchunk}

Emacs's [[M-n q]] is one command that means two things: fill the
prose, or re-indent the code, whichever this chunk is.  [[:NowebFillChunk]]
is the same command.  It exists alongside [[gqic]] and [[=ic]]---which
now work---because saying \enquote{tidy this chunk} without first
deciding which kind it is turns out to be what one actually wants
while writing.

<<[[noweb#fill_chunk]]>>=
" :NowebFillChunk -- Emacs's M-n q: fill the prose or re-indent the
" code, according to the chunk the cursor is in.
function! noweb#fill_chunk() abort
  let l:c = s:chunk_bounds(line('.'))
  if l:c.last < l:c.body
    return
  endif
  let l:view = winsaveview()
  try
    if l:c.kind ==# 'code'
      <<re-indent the code chunk>>
    else
      call s:format_piece({'kind': 'doc', 'from': l:c.body, 'to': l:c.last})
    endif
  finally
    call winrestview(l:view)
  endtry
endfunction
@

Re-indenting needs an indenter, and this buffer has none: its filetype
is [[noweb]], and \cref{sec:chunkopts} deliberately leaves
[['indentexpr']] out of the options a chunk gets, because an indent
script is not a value one can copy---it is code, holding buffer-local
state under guards this buffer never ran.  A bare [[=]] here therefore
falls through to Vim's built-in C indenting, which on a Python chunk
is worse than doing nothing.

There are two better answers, and which is available depends on the
editor.  The shadow (\cref{sec:shadowindent}) is the good one: the
chunk's code, in a real buffer of the right filetype, spliced where it
actually goes.  Without a shadow the same idea works one step less
well---copy the chunk into a throwaway buffer of its filetype, let
that filetype's indent script run there, and copy the indentation
back.  What it loses is context: the chunk is indented as though it
stood alone, because standing alone is all a chunk does until
something tangles it.  Only where the language is unknown does plain
[[=]] remain, and there Vim's default is all anyone can mean.

<<re-indent the code chunk>>=
let l:ft = get(noweb#languages(), l:c.name, '')
if s:shadow_indent(l:c.body, l:c.last)
  " The shadow's indenter had the whole tangle to work with.
elseif l:ft !=# ''
  call s:scratch_indent(l:ft, l:c.body, l:c.last)
else
  call s:normal_over(l:c.body, l:c.last, '=')
endif
@

The throwaway buffer takes the [[.nw]] buffer's indentation
conventions with it, or a literate program written with spaces would
come back with tabs in it.  It declines when the filetype turns out to
have no indenter at all, and the caller does nothing rather than
inventing one.

<<[[s:scratch_indent]]>>=
" Re-indent first..last with the indent script of filetype ft, run in
" a throwaway buffer of that filetype -- the fallback for when no
" shadow covers the chunk.  Returns 0 if ft has no indenter.
function! s:scratch_indent(ft, first, last) abort
  let l:lines = getline(a:first, a:last)
  let l:done = []
  let l:conventions = [&l:expandtab, &l:shiftwidth, &l:tabstop, &l:softtabstop]
  let l:eventignore = &eventignore
  set eventignore=all
  try
    silent keepalt new
    setlocal buftype=nofile noswapfile
    set eventignore=Syntax
    execute 'setfiletype' a:ft
    if &l:indentexpr ==# '' && &l:equalprg ==# '' && !&l:cindent && !&l:lisp
      return 0
    endif
    let [&l:expandtab, &l:shiftwidth, &l:tabstop, &l:softtabstop]
          \ = l:conventions
    call setline(1, l:lines)
    silent normal! gg=G
    let l:done = getline(1, '$')
  catch
    let l:done = []
  finally
    set eventignore=all
    silent! bwipeout!
    let &eventignore = l:eventignore
  endtry
  if len(l:done) != len(l:lines)
    return 0
  endif
  call setline(a:first, l:done)
  return 1
endfunction
@

\subsection{Editing options that follow the cursor}
\label{sec:chunkopts}

Here is the gap this section exists for.  In a Python chunk,
[['commentstring']] is still [[%\ %s]], so a commenting plugin
comments the line out the \TeX{} way; [['comments']] is \TeX{}'s, so
[[gq]] over a run of [[#]] comments does not know they have a leader;
[['iskeyword']] is \TeX{}'s, so [[*]] on [[foo_bar]] searches for
[[foo]].  The syntax says Python and the language server says Python;
only the editor's own behaviour still says \TeX{}.

The obvious repair is the Emacs one---switch the filetype as the
cursor moves---and it is not available.  Emacs can switch major modes
under point because it marks its own state [[permanent-local]], so the
switch does not take the minor mode with it; Vim has no such thing.  A
[[:setfiletype python]] in this buffer would run [[b:undo_ftplugin]]
and take with it the omnifunc, the tagfunc, the tag stack behaviour,
every map in \cref{sec:editmaps}, the shadow autocommands and the
syntax---the whole plugin.  What is wanted is not the filetype but the
handful of \emph{options} a filetype plugin sets, so we take those and
leave the filetype alone.

<<the per-chunk options>>=
" The buffer-local options a chunk's language should own.  Not
" 'textwidth': ftplugins mostly leave it alone and users mostly do
" not, so it belongs to the user, not to the chunk.
let s:CHUNK_OPTIONS = ['commentstring', 'comments', 'iskeyword',
      \ 'matchpairs', 'suffixesadd', 'formatoptions']
let s:optcache = {}
@

Where do a filetype's values come from?  From the filetype, by asking
it---the same answer \cref{sec:askeditor} gave for
\enquote{what language is this file?}, and the same throwaway buffer
to ask it in.  The difference is which events are wanted:
[[s:detect_in_scratch]] suppresses [[FileType]], because it only wants
detection, whereas here [[FileType]] is the entire point and it is
[[Syntax]] that is suppressed---loading a syntax file to read
[['commentstring']] would be a great deal of work for nothing.  Once
per language per session, cached.

<<[[s:options_for]]>>=
" The chunk options a filetype's ftplugin sets, read out of a
" throwaway buffer of that filetype.  An empty ft gives the values a
" buffer has when no filetype has spoken for it, which is how
" s:doc_options tells a deliberate value from a factory one.  Cached:
" this runs once per language per session.
function! s:options_for(ft) abort
  if has_key(s:optcache, a:ft)
    return s:optcache[a:ft]
  endif
  let l:opts = {}
  let l:eventignore = &eventignore
  set eventignore=all
  try
    silent keepalt new
    setlocal buftype=nofile noswapfile
    set eventignore=Syntax
    if a:ft !=# ''
      execute 'setfiletype' a:ft
    endif
    for l:o in s:CHUNK_OPTIONS
      let l:opts[l:o] = getbufvar('%', '&' . l:o)
    endfor
  catch
    let l:opts = {}
  finally
    set eventignore=all
    silent! bwipeout!
    let &eventignore = l:eventignore
  endtry
  let s:optcache[a:ft] = l:opts
  return l:opts
endfunction
@

The prose needs values too, and its filetype---[[noweb]]---is this
buffer's own, so they are mostly just what the options already are
when the plugin first looks.  Capturing them lazily rather than at
ftplugin time is deliberate: VimTeX initializes after us
(\cref{sec:vimtexprose}) and may set some of them, and by waiting until the
cursor first moves we record what the user really ended up with.

Mostly, but not entirely.  Filetype [[noweb]] has no ftplugin of its
own beyond this plugin's, so on a bare Vim nothing has ever told the
buffer that its prose is \TeX{}---[['commentstring']] is still Vim's
factory [[/*\ %s\ */]], which is a C comment in a \TeX{} document.
Where nothing has spoken for an option, the [[tex]] filetype should.

\enquote{Nothing has spoken for it} needs a definition, and
\enquote{it is empty} is not one---Vim ships non-empty defaults for
some of these and Neovim ships empty ones for the same, which is
exactly the sort of difference that makes a plugin work on one editor
and not the other.  The test that holds on both is to compare against
a buffer with \emph{no} filetype at all: an option still equal to what
a bare buffer would have is one nobody has claimed, and \TeX{}'s value
is better than the factory's.  Anything else---a value VimTeX chose, a
value from the user's own [[ftplugin/noweb.vim]]---was set
deliberately and stands.

<<[[s:doc_options]]>>=
" The prose's own options.  An option still holding the value a
" filetype-less buffer would have is one nothing has claimed, and
" takes filetype tex's instead -- the prose is TeX, and no ftplugin
" says so on a bare Vim.  Everything else was set deliberately (by
" VimTeX, or by the user) and stands.  Captured on first use, after
" every other ftplugin has had its say.
function! s:doc_options() abort
  if !exists('b:noweb_doc_options')
    let l:bare = s:options_for('')
    let l:tex = s:options_for('tex')
    let b:noweb_doc_options = {}
    for l:o in s:CHUNK_OPTIONS
      let l:v = getbufvar('%', '&' . l:o)
      let b:noweb_doc_options[l:o] =
            \ l:v ==# get(l:bare, l:o, l:v) ? get(l:tex, l:o, l:v) : l:v
    endfor
  endif
  return b:noweb_doc_options
endfunction
@

The hook itself runs on every cursor movement, so it is written to do
nothing at all in the common case.  [[s:chunk_bounds]]
(\cref{sec:bounds}) answers from its memo while the cursor stays in
one chunk, and the filetype it resolves to is compared against the one
already applied before any option is written.  Moving about inside a
chunk therefore costs a dictionary lookup and a string comparison.

<<[[noweb#chunk_options]]>>=
" Give the buffer the editing options of the chunk under the cursor.
" Called on every cursor movement, so the unchanged case must be
" nearly free.
function! noweb#chunk_options() abort
  if !get(g:, 'noweb_chunk_options', 1)
    return
  endif
  let l:c = s:chunk_bounds(line('.'))
  let l:ft = l:c.kind ==# 'code'
        \ ? get(noweb#languages(), l:c.name, '') : ''
  if get(b:, 'noweb_applied', v:null) is# l:ft
    return
  endif
  call s:doc_options()
  let l:opts = l:ft ==# '' ? {} : s:options_for(l:ft)
  " An unknown language is no reason to leave TeX's idea of a comment
  " in place, but it is no reason to invent one either: fall back to
  " the prose's options, which is where we came from.
  for [l:o, l:v] in items(empty(l:opts) ? b:noweb_doc_options : l:opts)
    call setbufvar('%', '&' . l:o, l:v)
  endfor
  let b:noweb_applied = l:ft
endfunction
@

Undoing this on a filetype change is the same loop against the
captured prose values, and it has to run: leaving a buffer that is no
longer noweb with some chunk's [['iskeyword']] would be a puzzling
thing to debug.

<<[[noweb#chunk_options_reset]]>>=
" Put the prose's options back; b:undo_ftplugin calls this.
function! noweb#chunk_options_reset() abort
  for [l:o, l:v] in items(get(b:, 'noweb_doc_options', {}))
    call setbufvar('%', '&' . l:o, l:v)
  endfor
  unlet! b:noweb_doc_options b:noweb_applied
endfunction
@

\subsection{Folding a chunk out of the way}
\label{sec:folding}

Emacs's answer to \enquote{let me see this chunk and nothing else} is
narrowing: [[M-n n]] restricts the buffer to the current chunk, [[M-n
N]] to the pair, and [[M-n t]] makes every navigation command re-narrow
after it moves.  Vim has no narrowing, and the honest translation is
not to build one---it is folding, which the editor already has, which
composes with everything, and which is reversible with a keystroke
rather than a mode.  [[zM]] then [[zv]] is [[M-n t]]; [[zj]] and
[[zk]] become chunk motions for free.

A [['foldexpr']] is called once per line, on every line, whenever the
folds are recomputed, so it must not think.  This one does not: it
tests the line against [[s:HEAD]] and says either \enquote{a fold
starts here} or \enquote{still inside one}.  Notably it does
\emph{not} consult [[s:chunk_bounds]]---and it gets the [[@ %def]]
case right anyway, because those lines are not in [[s:HEAD]] and so
stay inside the fold of the chunk they belong to.

<<[[noweb#foldexpr]]>>=
" 'foldexpr': one fold per chunk.  Runs per line and looks at nothing
" but the line; "@ %def" is not a head, so it folds with its chunk.
function! noweb#foldexpr(lnum) abort
  return getline(a:lnum) =~# s:HEAD ? '>1' : '1'
endfunction
@

The fold's one line of text is a chance to say something the fold has
hidden.  For a code chunk that is its name and its language---which
the narrowing it replaces could never have told anyone, since Emacs
does not know the language.

<<[[noweb#foldtext]]>>=
" 'foldtext': the chunk's name and inferred language, or the prose's
" first real line.
function! noweb#foldtext() abort
  let l:head = getline(v:foldstart)
  let l:count = v:foldend - v:foldstart + 1
  if l:head =~# s:DEFLINE
    let l:name = matchstr(l:head, s:DEF)
    let l:lang = get(noweb#languages(), l:name, '')
    let l:what = '@<<' . l:name . '@>>=' . (l:lang ==# '' ? '' : '  ' . l:lang)
  else
    <<describe a folded documentation chunk>>
  endif
  return printf('%s  [%d lines]', l:what, l:count)
endfunction
@

A documentation chunk has no name, so it is described by its first
line with anything to say---skipping the bare [[@]] marker and any
blank lines under it.

<<describe a folded documentation chunk>>=
let l:what = ''
for l:n in range(v:foldstart, min([v:foldend, v:foldstart + 4]))
  let l:text = substitute(getline(l:n), '^@\s*', '', '')
  if l:text !~# '^\s*$'
    let l:what = l:text
    break
  endif
endfor
if l:what ==# ''
  let l:what = '@'
endif
@

\subsection{Reaching a chunk by name}
\label{sec:goto}

[[<C-]>]] (\cref{sec:tagfunc}) answers \enquote{where is
\emph{this}?}, with the cursor already on the name.  Emacs's
[[noweb-goto-chunk]] answers a different question---\enquote{where is
the one called \dots?}---by prompting with completion over every chunk
in the buffer, and this plugin has had no equivalent.  It cannot
simply be [[:tag]]: Vim's [[:tag]] completes from a tags \emph{file},
and never consults [['tagfunc']].

<<[[noweb#goto]]>>=
" :NowebGoto -- jump to a chunk's first definition by name, defaulting
" to the one under the cursor.  Emacs lands on the last chunk of the
" name, an accident of how it builds its alist; the first definition
" is what anyone means.
function! noweb#goto(name) abort
  let l:name = a:name ==# '' ? s:chunk_at_cursor() : a:name
  if l:name ==# ''
    call s:no_chunk()
    return
  endif
  let l:defs = get(s:chunk_occurrences(), l:name, {'defs': []}).defs
  if empty(l:defs)
    echohl WarningMsg
    echomsg 'noweb: no definition of @<<' . l:name . '@>>'
    echohl None
    return
  endif
  <<push the tag stack, then jump>>
endfunction
@

Arriving by [[<C-]>]] and arriving by [[:NowebGoto]] should leave
equally well, so the jump is pushed onto the tag stack and
\texttt{CTRL-T} comes back from either.  [[settagstack]] is younger
than the rest of what the plugin needs, so the [['']] mark carries the
return trip on its own where it is missing.

<<push the tag stack, then jump>>=
normal! m'
if exists('*settagstack')
  call settagstack(win_getid(), {'items': [{'tagname': l:name,
        \ 'from': [bufnr('%'), line('.'), col('.'), 0]}]}, 'a')
endif
call cursor(l:defs[0][0], 1)
@

Completing the name is where noweb's willingness to let chunk names
contain blanks stops being free.  Vim hands a completion function only
the text since the last blank, and substitutes the chosen candidate
for exactly that much, so returning whole names would repeat
everything before the final word.  Matching on the whole argument and
returning only its tail puts the two ends back in agreement.

The two callers differ only in where the name starts on the line: a
command has its own name in front, and an [[input()]] prompt does not.

<<[[noweb#chunk_names]]>>=
" Chunk names starting with 'lead', each trimmed to the part Vim will
" substitute for a:arglead.  Names may contain blanks while a:arglead
" is only the text since the last one, so the two must be given
" separately or completing "greet body" would insert "greet greet
" body".
function! s:name_completions(lead, arglead) abort
  let l:drop = len(a:lead) - len(a:arglead)
  let l:names = sort(filter(keys(s:chunk_occurrences()),
        \ {_, n -> stridx(n, a:lead) == 0}))
  return map(l:names, {_, n -> strpart(n, l:drop)})
endfunction

" Completion for :NowebGoto: the command's own name comes first.
function! noweb#chunk_names(arglead, cmdline, cursorpos) abort
  return s:name_completions(
        \ matchstr(a:cmdline, '^\s*\S\+\s\+\zs.*'), a:arglead)
endfunction

" Completion for input(): the whole line is the name.
function! noweb#chunk_name_input(arglead, cmdline, cursorpos) abort
  return s:name_completions(a:cmdline, a:arglead)
endfunction
@

[[:NowebChunks]] is the survey that goes with the jump: every
definition in the file, in file order, in the location list---a table
of contents for the program, next to VimTeX's table of contents for
the prose.  It is the location list rather than the quickfix list for
the same reason [[:NowebRefs]] is (\cref{sec:refs}): a survey belongs
to the window that asked for it.

<<[[noweb#chunks]]>>=
" :NowebChunks -- every chunk definition in file order, in the
" location list.
function! noweb#chunks() abort
  let l:occ = s:chunk_occurrences()
  let l:items = []
  for l:name in keys(l:occ)
    for l:def in l:occ[l:name].defs
      call add(l:items, {'bufnr': bufnr('%'), 'lnum': l:def[0],
            \ 'col': l:def[1], 'text': '@<<' . l:name . '@>>='})
    endfor
  endfor
  if empty(l:items)
    echohl WarningMsg
    echomsg 'noweb: no chunks in this buffer'
    echohl None
    return
  endif
  call sort(l:items, {a, b -> a.lnum - b.lnum})
  call setloclist(0, [], ' ', {'title': 'noweb chunks', 'items': l:items})
  lopen
endfunction
@

\subsection{Starting a chunk}
\label{sec:newchunk}

Emacs offers three ways to begin a chunk: [[M-n i]] prompts for a name
and drops in a template, and the electric [[@]] and [[<]] keys turn
those characters at the start of a line into the beginnings of a
documentation or code chunk.  The electric keys are not ported.
Rebinding [[<]] in insert mode to guess at intent is a hostile thing
to do in Vim, and the useful half of what they buy---getting the name
right---is already covered better by completing it after
[[@<<]] (\cref{sec:complete}).

The template is worth having, and worth completing the name of too:
noweb's most common editing act is adding an \emph{append} to a chunk
that already exists, which is the same template with a name from the
list.  What it inserts differs from Emacs's in two ways.  It leaves
out the [[@ %def ]] line, which noweb only needs when a chunk should
appear in the identifier index and which an empty one clutters the
source with; and it closes the chunk with [[@]], so what lands in the
buffer is complete and valid rather than something the writer has to
remember to finish.  The leading [[@]] appears only when there is code
to get out of first.

<<[[noweb#new_chunk]]>>=
" :NowebNewChunk -- open a chunk below the cursor, with the cursor on
" its first body line.  With no name, prompt for one with completion,
" so appending to an existing chunk is the same gesture.
function! noweb#new_chunk(name) abort
  let l:name = a:name
  if l:name ==# ''
    let l:name = input('Chunk name: ', '',
          \ 'customlist,noweb#chunk_name_input')
    redraw
  endif
  if l:name ==# ''
    return
  endif
  let l:at = line('.')
  let l:lines = s:chunk_bounds(l:at).kind ==# 'code' ? ['@', ''] : []
  call append(l:at, l:lines + ['@<<' . l:name . '@>>=', '', '@'])
  call cursor(l:at + len(l:lines) + 2, 1)
endfunction
@

\subsection{Binding the objects and the motions}
\label{sec:editmaps}

Ten new keys is a lot to take from a user's buffer, so they follow the
discipline \cref{sec:ftplugin} set for \texttt{]c} and \texttt{[c}:
every one goes through a [[<Plug>]] name, [[g:noweb_no_maps]]
suppresses the lot, and [[hasmapto]] yields to anything the user has
already bound.  Only the [[<Plug>]] definitions are unconditional, and
they are global---rebinding them is how a user moves ours out of the
way.

The four objects each want two modes and the six motions want three,
so this is one place where writing the maps out longhand would be
forty lines of near-identical text; a loop over the table says the
same thing and keeps the correspondence visible.

<<the chunk objects and motions>>=
" ic / ac / iC / aC, in both the modes a text object serves.
for [s:key, s:plug, s:args] in [
      \ ['ic', 'inner-chunk', '0, 0'],
      \ ['ac', 'a-chunk', '0, 1'],
      \ ['iC', 'inner-pair', '1, 0'],
      \ ['aC', 'a-pair', '1, 1']]
  for s:mode in ['x', 'o']
    execute s:mode . 'noremap <silent> <Plug>(noweb-' . s:plug . ') '
          \ . ':<C-u>call noweb#textobj(' . s:args . ')<CR>'
    if !get(g:, 'noweb_no_maps', 0)
          \ && !hasmapto('<Plug>(noweb-' . s:plug . ')', s:mode)
      execute s:mode . 'map <buffer> ' . s:key
            \ . ' <Plug>(noweb-' . s:plug . ')'
    endif
  endfor
  let b:undo_ftplugin .= ' | silent! xunmap <buffer> ' . s:key
        \ . ' | silent! ounmap <buffer> ' . s:key
endfor

" ]] [[ ]m [m ][ [] -- structural movement, in the three modes a
" motion serves.
for [s:key, s:plug, s:args] in [
      \ [']]', 'next-chunk', "1, '', v:count1"],
      \ ['[[', 'prev-chunk', "-1, '', v:count1"],
      \ [']m', 'next-code-chunk', "1, 'code', v:count1"],
      \ ['[m', 'prev-code-chunk', "-1, 'code', v:count1"],
      \ ['][', 'next-doc-chunk', "1, 'doc', v:count1"],
      \ ['[]', 'prev-doc-chunk', "-1, 'doc', v:count1"]]
  for s:mode in ['n', 'x', 'o']
    execute s:mode . 'noremap <silent> <Plug>(noweb-' . s:plug . ') '
          \ . ':<C-u>call noweb#next_chunk(' . s:args . ')<CR>'
    if !get(g:, 'noweb_no_maps', 0)
          \ && !hasmapto('<Plug>(noweb-' . s:plug . ')', s:mode)
      execute s:mode . 'map <buffer> ' . s:key
            \ . ' <Plug>(noweb-' . s:plug . ')'
    endif
  endfor
  let b:undo_ftplugin .= ' | silent! nunmap <buffer> ' . s:key
        \ . ' | silent! xunmap <buffer> ' . s:key
        \ . ' | silent! ounmap <buffer> ' . s:key
endfor

unlet! s:key s:plug s:args s:mode
@

The formatting hook takes no keys at all: [[gq]] and [[gw]] already
route through [['formatexpr']], so setting it is the whole
installation.

<<the formatting hook and the [[:NowebFillChunk]] command>>=
setlocal formatexpr=noweb#format()
command! -buffer NowebFillChunk call noweb#fill_chunk()
let b:undo_ftplugin .= ' | setlocal formatexpr<'
      \ . ' | silent! delcommand -buffer NowebFillChunk'
@

The options hook needs an autocommand, and one that fires often; the
work of making that cheap was done in \cref{sec:bounds,sec:chunkopts}.
[[BufEnter]] joins the cursor events so that returning to the buffer
in a different window lands with the right options rather than the
ones the other window left behind.

<<follow the cursor with the chunk's options>>=
if get(g:, 'noweb_chunk_options', 1)
  augroup nowebChunkOptions
    autocmd! * <buffer>
    autocmd CursorMoved,CursorMovedI,BufEnter <buffer>
          \ call noweb#chunk_options()
  augroup END
  let b:undo_ftplugin .= ' | silent! autocmd! nowebChunkOptions * <buffer>'
        \ . ' | call noweb#chunk_options_reset()'
endif
@

Folding is the one thing here that is off unless asked for.  A
[['foldexpr']] runs on every line of the buffer, and---more to the
point---how a buffer folds is a matter of taste that users hold
firmly.  [[g:noweb_fold]] turns it on.

<<fold by chunk when asked>>=
if get(g:, 'noweb_fold', 0)
  setlocal foldmethod=expr
  setlocal foldexpr=noweb#foldexpr(v:lnum)
  setlocal foldtext=noweb#foldtext()
  let b:undo_ftplugin .= ' | setlocal foldmethod< foldexpr< foldtext<'
endif
@

The three chunk commands need no keys either.  [[:NowebGoto]] takes an
optional name and completes it; [[:NowebNewChunk]] takes an optional
name and prompts when it is missing, so [[:NowebNewChunk]] on its own
is the interactive form and [[:NowebNewChunk parser]] the direct one.

<<the chunk commands>>=
command! -buffer -nargs=? -complete=customlist,noweb#chunk_names
      \ NowebGoto call noweb#goto(<q-args>)
command! -buffer NowebChunks call noweb#chunks()
command! -buffer -nargs=? NowebNewChunk call noweb#new_chunk(<q-args>)
let b:undo_ftplugin .= ' | silent! delcommand -buffer NowebGoto'
      \ . ' | silent! delcommand -buffer NowebChunks'
      \ . ' | silent! delcommand -buffer NowebNewChunk'
@

\section{The help file}
\label{sec:helpfile}

Emacs has had one thing this plugin never did: a way to ask it what it
can do.  [[M-n h]] describes the mode, and the docstring it prints
(\texttt{noweb-mode.el} lines 157--207) is a grouped cheat sheet of
every key.  Vim's equivalent is a help file, and until now the plugin
shipped none---a user's only documentation was the README and this
document, neither of which is in the editor when the question arises.

[[doc/noweb.txt]] is the plugin's eighth tangled file, written here
like everything else so that it cannot drift from the code it
describes.  It is a reference, not a retelling: the reasoning lives in
this document and the help file says what the keys and options are.
Vim builds the tag index itself when the plugin manager runs
[[:helptags]].

<<[[doc/noweb.txt]]>>=
*noweb.txt*     Literate programming with noweb            *noweb* *vim-noweb*

Highlighting, navigation and editing for noweb literate programs
(*.nw): TeX prose, noweb markup, and every code chunk in the language
it is actually written in.

1. Requirements ......................... |noweb-requirements|
2. Highlighting ......................... |noweb-highlighting|
3. Completion and navigation ............ |noweb-navigation|
4. Editing in chunks .................... |noweb-editing|
5. Language intelligence ................ |noweb-intelligence|
6. Commands ............................. |noweb-commands|
7. Options .............................. |noweb-options|
8. Mappings ............................. |noweb-mappings|

==============================================================================
1. Requirements                                          *noweb-requirements*

Highlighting, completion, navigation and everything in
|noweb-editing| need nothing but Vim.

|noweb-intelligence| needs Neovim and the "linemark" and "nolinemap"
tools from the dbosk noweb fork (contrib/dbosk).  "notangle" and
"nolinemap" must be on $PATH, because the plugin runs them itself;
"linemark" need only be installed where noweb keeps its filters (its
LIB directory, which "make install" in contrib/dbosk writes to),
since it is only ever reached as a "notangle -filter" and notangle
puts LIB on $PATH for its filters.

A language server per language is optional; without one the tangled
preview, |:NowebMake| and a pattern-based `gd` still work.

==============================================================================
2. Highlighting                                          *noweb-highlighting*

The whole buffer is TeX prose by default.  Chunk headers, chunk
references and [[...]] quotes are marked as noweb markup, and each
code chunk's body is highlighted in the chunk's own language.

The language is inferred the way noweb's "autolang" weave filter does
it: from filename-like chunk names, from #! lines, and by propagation
along @<<use@>> edges, so a descriptively named chunk used by a typed
root inherits the root's language.  Name and shebang lookups ask the
editor's own filetype database, so every language the editor can
detect is covered.

Highlighting refreshes on every write.  |:NowebSyncLang| refreshes it
on demand.  |g:noweb_langrules| adds name-pattern rules.

==============================================================================
3. Completion and navigation                              *noweb-navigation*

                                                        *noweb-completion*
Chunk names complete after "@<<" through the buffer's 'omnifunc':
|i_CTRL-X_CTRL-O| in plain Vim, and automatically in YouCompleteMe and
any framework with an omnifunc bridge.  Completion reads the live
buffer, so unsaved chunks are offered the moment they are typed.  It
closes the "@>>" unless it is already there, and the menu marks
referenced-but-undefined names -- a quick way to spot a typo.  At a
definition position the not-yet-defined names sort first.

                                                        *noweb-tags*
Chunk definitions resolve as tags through 'tagfunc'.  |CTRL-]| on a
reference jumps to the definition, |:tnext| steps through the appends
(a chunk defined several times has several tag matches), |CTRL-T|
jumps back and |g]| lists them all.

                                                        *noweb-occurrences*
]c and [c step through every occurrence -- definition or use -- of the
chunk under the cursor, wrapping.  |:NowebRefs| collects them all in
the location list.  |:NowebGoto| jumps to a chunk by name, and
|:NowebChunks| lists every definition in the file.

==============================================================================
4. Editing in chunks                                          *noweb-editing*

                                                        *noweb-textobjects*
Four linewise text objects, in operator-pending and visual mode:

    ic      the chunk's content, without its header or its trailing
            "@ %def" index declarations
    ac      the whole chunk, markup included
    iC      the content of the documentation/code pair
    aC      the whole documentation/code pair

A "pair" is noweb's unit of composition: a passage of prose and the
code chunk it introduces.  From a code chunk, aC reaches back over the
prose; from a documentation chunk, forward over the code.

                                                        *noweb-motions*
Six motions, all wrapping, all taking a count, all setting the |''|
mark so that |CTRL-O| returns:

    ]]      next chunk of either kind        [[      previous
    ]m      next code chunk                  [m      previous
    ][      next documentation chunk         []      previous

                                                        *noweb-formatting*
|gq| and |gw| understand chunks.  A range covering several chunks is
split at the boundaries and each piece filled inside its own chunk, so
"gqG" never joins a paragraph onto a chunk header; and because a sweep
like that means "tidy the prose", code chunks in a multi-chunk range
are left alone.  A range inside a single chunk is taken as deliberate
and formatted whatever it is, so gqip over a run of comments in a code
chunk rewraps them with their leaders.

Blanks inside [[...]] quotes are protected for the duration of the
fill, so a quote is never broken across two lines.

|:NowebFillChunk| formats the whole chunk the cursor is in: filling it
if it is prose, re-indenting it if it is code.

                                                        *noweb-chunk-options*
The buffer's editing options follow the cursor.  In a code chunk,
'commentstring', 'comments', 'iskeyword', 'matchpairs', 'suffixesadd'
and 'formatoptions' are the chunk language's own, so commenting
plugins insert the right leader, |star| respects the language's word
characters, and comment-aware |gq| works.  In prose they are TeX's.
Set |g:noweb_chunk_options| to 0 to switch this off.

                                                        *noweb-folding*
With |g:noweb_fold| set, 'foldmethod' is "expr" and each chunk is one
fold, labelled with its name and inferred language.  "zM" then "zv" is
the closest thing Vim has to narrowing to a chunk.

==============================================================================
5. Language intelligence                                 *noweb-intelligence*

Neovim only.  Every typed root chunk -- one named like a file, defined
but never used -- gets an invisible tangled shadow buffer, kept in
sync with the live .nw and served to a language server.  Results map
back into the noweb source:

- diagnostics appear on the offending chunk lines as you type;
- completion inside a chunk comes from the language server, through
  the same 'omnifunc';
- `K` shows hover documentation and `gd` jumps to a definition,
  landing in the right chunk even across chunks.  With no language
  server, `gd` falls back to language-aware definition patterns over
  the whole tangle;
- |:NowebFillChunk| re-indents a code chunk with the language's own
  indent script, run in the shadow;
- |:NowebTangled| opens the tangled code read-only in a split, cursor
  synced in both directions;
- |:NowebMake| runs a command over the tangled file and fills the
  quickfix list with positions in the .nw.

Covered out of the box: the languages noweb ships "autodefs" filters
for -- Python, shell, make, C, C++, Haskell, Java, Rust, TeX, Icon,
Pascal, yacc -- plus Lua and Lean.  |g:noweb_languages| configures
them; |g:noweb_shadow| switches the whole subsystem off.

VimTeX, when installed, is initialized for the prose: motions, text
objects, table of contents and \cite/\ref completion, routed through
the same 'omnifunc'.  |g:noweb_vimtex| opts out.

==============================================================================
6. Commands                                                  *noweb-commands*

                                                        *:NowebGoto*
:NowebGoto [{name}]     Jump to the first definition of chunk {name},
                        completing the name; with no argument, the
                        chunk under the cursor.  Pushes the tag stack,
                        so |CTRL-T| comes back.

                                                        *:NowebChunks*
:NowebChunks            Every chunk definition in file order, in the
                        location list.

                                                        *:NowebRefs*
:NowebRefs              Every definition and use of the chunk under
                        the cursor, in the location list.

                                                        *:NowebNewChunk*
:NowebNewChunk [{name}] Open a chunk below the cursor and put the
                        cursor in its body.  With no argument, prompts
                        for the name with completion -- so appending
                        to an existing chunk is the same gesture.

                                                        *:NowebFillChunk*
:NowebFillChunk         Fill the current chunk if it is prose;
                        re-indent it if it is code.

                                                        *:NowebSyncLang*
:NowebSyncLang          Re-infer chunk languages and rebuild the
                        highlighting now, without writing.

                                                        *:NowebTangled*
:NowebTangled [{root}]  Open the tangled code for {root} read-only in
                        a split, cursor-synced with the source; run it
                        again to close.  Takes |:command-modifiers|,
                        so ":vert NowebTangled" works.  Neovim only.

                                                        *:NowebMake*
:NowebMake {cmd}        Tangle the root under the cursor, run {cmd}
                        ("%" is the tangled file) through nolinemap,
                        and fill the quickfix list with positions in
                        the .nw.  Needs a saved buffer.  Neovim only.

==============================================================================
7. Options                                                    *noweb-options*

                                                       *g:noweb_chunk_options*
g:noweb_chunk_options   (default 1)  Give the buffer the chunk
                        language's editing options as the cursor
                        moves.  See |noweb-chunk-options|.

                                                        *g:noweb_fold*
g:noweb_fold            (default 0)  Fold by chunk.

                                                        *g:noweb_no_maps*
g:noweb_no_maps         (default 0)  Install no default mappings at
                        all.  The |<Plug>| names stay; see
                        |noweb-mappings|.

                                                        *g:noweb_langrules*
g:noweb_langrules       (default [])  List of [pattern, filetype]
                        pairs deciding a chunk's language from its
                        name, tried before everything else, first
                        match winning.  Mirrors noweave's -langrule. >
                            let g:noweb_langrules =
                                  \ [['^check \[\[.*\.py\]\]', 'python']]
<
                                                        *g:noweb_languages*
g:noweb_languages       (default {})  Per-language configuration for
                        |noweb-intelligence|, deep-merged over the
                        defaults.  Keys are filetypes; each may set
                        "lsp" (the server command), "leader" (the
                        comment leader for linemark), "filetype" and
                        "definitions". >
                            let g:noweb_languages =
                                  \ #{ python: #{ lsp: ['pyright-langserver',
                                  \                     '--stdio'] } }
<
                                                        *g:noweb_shadow*
g:noweb_shadow          (default 1)  Enable |noweb-intelligence|.

                                                        *g:noweb_vimtex*
g:noweb_vimtex          (default 1)  Initialize VimTeX for the prose.

                                                       *g:noweb_tangled_split*
g:noweb_tangled_split   (default "rightbelow split")  Default split
                        for |:NowebTangled|.

                                                     *g:noweb_tangled_markers*
g:noweb_tangled_markers (default 0)  Show linemark's marker comments
                        in the |:NowebTangled| preview.

                                                      *g:noweb_scan_vimscript*
g:noweb_scan_vimscript  (default 0)  Use the Vim script occurrence
                        scan on Neovim too.  An escape hatch.

==============================================================================
8. Mappings                                                  *noweb-mappings*

Default mappings, all buffer-local:

    ic ac iC aC             |noweb-textobjects|
    ]] [[ ]m [m ][ []       |noweb-motions|
    ]c [c                   |noweb-occurrences|
    K                       hover documentation (Neovim)
    gd                      go to definition (Neovim)

|g:noweb_no_maps| suppresses all of them.  Any one of them is also
suppressed individually by binding its |<Plug>| name yourself: >

    nmap <buffer> <Leader>n <Plug>(noweb-next-chunk)
<
The names are:

    <Plug>(noweb-inner-chunk)       <Plug>(noweb-a-chunk)
    <Plug>(noweb-inner-pair)        <Plug>(noweb-a-pair)
    <Plug>(noweb-next-chunk)        <Plug>(noweb-prev-chunk)
    <Plug>(noweb-next-code-chunk)   <Plug>(noweb-prev-code-chunk)
    <Plug>(noweb-next-doc-chunk)    <Plug>(noweb-prev-doc-chunk)
    <Plug>(noweb-next-occurrence)   <Plug>(noweb-prev-occurrence)
    <Plug>(noweb-hover)             <Plug>(noweb-definition)

==============================================================================
