Two conventions keep the tangle byte-exact:

\begin{description}
\item[Escaping:] the plugin's own code is full of literal [[<<]] and
  [[>>]]---in the very patterns that match noweb markup.  Inside code
  chunks these are written [[@<<]] and [[@>>]], \emph{uniformly}, even
  where an unpaired bracket would happen to tangle correctly: a later
  edit adding a second bracket on the same line would silently pair
  them into a chunk reference.  (The nastiest case is the comment
  mentioning [[cat @<<EOF @>> log]]: unescaped, [[@<<EOF @>>]] is a
  well-formed reference to a chunk named \enquote{[[EOF]] }.)
\item[Tabs:] the header comment block of the syntax file contains
  tabs, and notangle expands tabs by default; [[-t8]] preserves them.
\end{description}

The Makefile is a plain committed file---it cannot tangle itself.  The
tangled [[.vim]] files are committed too, so the plugin works from a
bare clone; only the woven [[.tex]]/[[.pdf]] are ignored.  The weave
uses [[-autolang]] and the minted-based [[tominted]] filter, so the
chunks of this document are themselves highlighted as Vim script.

